---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ScoreLadder from '../components/ScoreLadder.astro';
import ScoreName from '../components/ScoreName.astro';
import { IS_PROVISIONAL } from '../lib/maturity';
import { IS_PLACEHOLDER } from '../lib/distribution';
import { SECTIONS } from '../lib/questions';
import { MARK, EDITION, SCORE_SHORT, REPORT_NAME } from '../lib/brand';

/**
 * THE FINISH PAGE. ONE PAGE, WHERE THERE USED TO BE TWO.
 *
 * Dan, 08:12: *"I think this should be the finish page when they're done. Say thank you. Give them
 * their score... so it's like, thank you for taking the survey, get your sneak peek, and then
 * below, it's kind of like their audit."* And on the agency block, 08:02: *"I would probably put
 * the join the agency on this page... work the agency into that top part, so it's like sneak peek
 * of Baseout, and then for agency it has this as like a sidebar."*
 *
 * The order down the page has since moved once more, on Dan's own review of it: thank you and the
 * score, then the detail the score was cut out of, then the rest of the report, and the sneak peek
 * with the agency offer sits in 05 among the other things that happen next. `/sneak-peek` is a
 * redirect into this page; it was a second screen behind a button, and a respondent who closed the
 * tab on the thank-you never saw the thing that was ready.
 *
 * THE SCORE IS A NUMBER NOW. `maturity.ts` computes the DOAP Score out of 100 from the bank's
 * canonical six-question core index, and the band is the bucket the number falls in. Both the
 * per-option points and the bucket edges are provisional until Dan writes Appendix B, so
 * `IS_PROVISIONAL` puts a marker beside the number rather than letting it pass as settled.
 *
 * The scoring seam is still a prototype: the score is computed client-side from the answers
 * `/survey` leaves in localStorage. The backend team replaces it with one returned by
 * `/api/submit`.
 */

/**
 * ONE ENDING, AND IT IS THE ISSUED REPORT.
 *
 * This page carried three drawings of the same result behind `?v=a|b|c` while Oleh chose between
 * them, plus a chip switcher in the chrome to flip between them. He chose the ruled report on
 * 2026-08-28 and the other two are gone: the markup, the CSS, the two motion systems and the
 * switcher. `?v=` is no longer read at all, so a stale `?v=a` in somebody's history renders this
 * ending rather than a blank page.
 *
 * The numeral hangs ON the rule that divides one section from the next — a chapter mark rather
 * than a margin note. That was `?v=c`'s `data-numerals="rule"`; with one treatment left the
 * attribute is a switch with one position, so the treatment is written into `.rep-sec` itself.
 */

/** The date the reader received this. Rendered per request, so it is never a stale literal. */
const issued = new Intl.DateTimeFormat('en-GB', {
  day: 'numeric',
  month: 'long',
  year: 'numeric',
}).format(new Date());

const token = Astro.url.searchParams.get('t') ?? 'preview';
const agency = Astro.url.searchParams.get('a') === '1';
/** The screener's exit. Bank S1: these responses are excluded from the core dataset. */
const screenedOut = Astro.url.searchParams.get('s') === '1';
---

<Layout title={`Your ${SCORE_SHORT}: ${MARK}`} noindex>
  <main>
    {screenedOut ? (
      /* The screened-out ending. No score, because nothing was measured: the bank sends these
         responses to "a thank-you screen and an optional email for the report" and excludes them
         from the dataset. Pretending to score them would put a number on six unanswered questions. */
      <section class="finish-out bo-measure">
        <p class="bo-eyebrow">Recorded</p>
        <h1 class="bo-display bo-display--sm">Thank you for taking the survey</h1>
        <p class="bo-lede">
          This one is built for teams running bases day to day, so we stopped early rather than ask
          you about restore times you have never needed. We will still send you {REPORT_NAME} when
          it publishes.
        </p>
      </section>
    ) : (
      <article class="rep">
        <!-- ── THE MASTHEAD. What this is, whose copy it is, and when it was issued. ─────────── -->
        <header class="rep-mast">
          <span class="rep-mast__mark">{EDITION}</span>
          <span class="rep-mast__meta">Your copy &middot; {issued}</span>
        </header>

        <!-- ── THE OPENING ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
             The thank-you no longer promises an email and stops. It says what this page IS, because
             the page IS the short report: Oleh, 2026-08-27, "по факту він на цій сторінці бачить
             вже короткий репорт". The email keeps a small line here and a panel of its own in 05,
             where it belongs among the other things that happen next. -->
        <div class="rep-open bo-measure">
          {/*
            THE EYEBROW NAMES THE DOCUMENT NOW, WHICH IS THE WHOLE POINT OF AN EYEBROW.

            It said "Recorded", which is what the `h1` under it already says: thank you for taking
            the survey means the answers are in. Two lines, one fact, and the page never stated what
            it IS — while promising a "report" by email three inches below, so the only name on
            offer belonged to the other document.

            Dan, 2026-08-28, on this exact block: *"this should kind of be like 'your DOAP report
            summary' or something, just to separate this information in here"*, and then the rule
            that settles the noun: *"maybe we don't call this a report; the report is more the
            industry overall one."*

            SUMMARY HERE, ANALYSIS BELOW, and the two words are doing different work rather than
            being alternatives. This block IS the summary — thank-you, the number, the band, one
            sentence — and `#rep-jump` in 01 points down at the analysis that explains it. Naming
            both "Your DOAP Score analysis" put the same four words twice on one screen and told the
            reader nothing about which was which. `screenedOut` above keeps "Recorded": nothing was
            scored there, so there is neither a summary nor an analysis to name.
          */}
          {/*
            ── THE OPENING WAS NINE THINGS DEEP AND IS NOW SIX ─────────────────────────────────

            Oleh, 2026-08-31: *"this block with all the text feels overloaded. Think about how to
            optimise it, make it shorter, there's just a lot of everything."* He is counting: before
            this pass the reader met an eyebrow, a heading, a three-line lede, a two-line aside, a
            second eyebrow, the number, the band, a blurb and a comparison — nine stacked elements
            before the page gave up its finding.

            Three came out, and each because something else on screen already carries it:

              THE LEDE'S FIRST SENTENCE listed what the survey is about ("how you protect, change
              and document the bases your business runs on"). The reader spent fifteen minutes on
              exactly that; it is the one paragraph on this page that tells them nothing they did
              not just supply. What survives is the half that cannot be inferred and is the actual
              claim this page rests on: every number is read back, not computed about them.

              THE REPORT ASIDE promised the published report by email. Section 01 begins directly
              under this block and its "By email" column says the same thing with the address it is
              going to. That was written when the aside was three screens away from it.

              THE SECOND EYEBROW, "The DOAP Score", labelled a 5.5rem numeral that carries `/ 100`,
              sits above its own band name and beside a ladder captioned "Where you landed". It was
              the third time the score was named inside 200px — and the third only exists because
              the FIRST one does, which is Dan's ask and stays.

            Nothing that was a finding was cut. The number, the band, the sentence that says what
            the band means and the comparison are all untouched.
          */}
          <p class="bo-eyebrow">Your {SCORE_SHORT} summary</p>
          <h1 class="rep-h1">Thank you for taking the survey</h1>
          <p class="bo-lede">Every number below is read back from what you told us.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- ── THE VERDICT. The number takes the room; the instrument answers it on the right. -->
        <section class="rep-verdict" id="result" hidden>
          <div>
            <p class="finish-number rep-number">
              <span id="result-score">0</span><span class="finish-number__of">/ 100</span>
              {IS_PROVISIONAL && (
                <span
                  class="bo-badge bo-badge--later"
                  title="The per-option points and the band cutoffs are a reading of an unwritten rubric: the report's Appendix B has not been agreed yet (open question O-2). The six questions scored are the bank's own canonical core index."
                >Provisional rubric</span>
              )}
            </p>
            <h2 class="rep-band" id="result-band"></h2>
            <p class="bo-verdict__blurb" id="result-blurb"></p>
            <p class="verdict-compare">
              <span id="result-compare"></span>
              {IS_PLACEHOLDER && (
                <span class="bo-badge bo-badge--later" title="No responses have been counted yet: /api/submit is a stub and the responses table does not exist (survey-app task 2.1). These shares are a shape, not a finding.">Sample data</span>
              )}
            </p>

            {/*
              THE "EMAIL ME MY REPORT" BUTTON USED TO SIT HERE, under the score. It moved into 01,
              where the other two things a reader can do already are: Oleh, 2026-08-28, *"the
              'Email me my report' button can go into the section we call What happens next — then
              the user has a call to action"*. The verdict block is the finding; the actions are one
              block, one screen down, and a lone ghost button under a 5.5rem numeral was the odd
              one out in both.
            */}
          </div>

          <div class="rep-instrument">
            {/*
              THE LADDER, NOT THE RULER. The tick ruler this replaced (`.bo-meter`, with each
              band's share printed under it) is gone: this is the banded form Oleh picked off the
              canvas — one continuous track filled left to right, divided where the bands divide,
              with the four names underneath. The per-band shares came off with it: they are
              placeholder figures, and the one comparison worth making is already a sentence beside
              the score.
            */}
            <ScoreLadder scored note="0 to 100">
              <Fragment slot="eyebrow">Where you landed</Fragment>
            </ScoreLadder>

            <!-- The sitting itself: how much they gave, which a grade never says back to them. -->
            <div class="rep-facts" id="rep-facts" hidden>
              <div><span class="rep-facts__n" id="rep-answered">0</span><span class="rep-facts__l">Questions answered</span></div>
              <div><span class="rep-facts__n">{SECTIONS.length}</span><span class="rep-facts__l">Sections</span></div>
              <div><span class="rep-facts__n">6</span><span class="rep-facts__l">Scored practices</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ── 01 · what happens next ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
             Every numbered section carries an id, 05 included. Four of them needed one anyway to be
             shown or hidden; this one never did, and it gets one so that whatever drives the scroll
             animation later has the same handle on all five rather than four and an exception. -->
        <section class="rep-sec" id="rep-next-sec">
          <span class="rep-sec__n">01</span>
          <div>
            <h2 class="rep-h2">What happens next</h2>
            {/*
              THINGS ON CLOCKS — the version Oleh picked off the canvas.

              The built one laid them out as tiles of equal weight, and they read as scattered
              because nothing on screen said what separates them. They are not interchangeable
              offers: one is ready NOW, one takes SECONDS, one arrives WHEN THE REPORT SHIPS, and
              one exists only for AGENCIES. The label above each column is that distinction, and it
              is the first thing in the column because it is the thing that orders them.

              THE MIDDLE COLUMN IS THE INBOX, AND IT HOLDS BOTH DELIVERIES. Oleh, 2026-08-28:
              *"the 'Email me my report' button can go into the section we call What happens
              next."*

              It was built as two columns first — one for the score email, one for the published
              report — because they ARE two documents and a button under a heading that says "The
              full report" reads as the thing that makes the published report arrive, or as a hoop
              to jump through to get it, and neither is true. OLEH — not Dan; an earlier version of
              this comment put his words in Dan's mouth, and the attribution matters now that Dan
              has asked for a fourth column of his own — looked at that and asked the better
              question: *"why is there a fourth column? I thought 'email me my report' and 'the full
              report' were the same thing."* He is wrong that they are one document and right that
              there is only one ADDRESS, given once, already recorded — and two columns each naming
              an inbox implied two separate asks. Worse, the labels above them, `Now` and `In
              seconds`, were the same moment written twice in a row that presents itself as a
              timeline.

              WHAT CAME BACK IS NOT THAT COLUMN. `#rep-jump` leads this row from 2026-08-28. It
              asks for nothing, names no inbox and delivers nothing — it points down the page at
              what the reader already has. What was collapsed was two columns competing for one
              address, and that stays collapsed.

              So: one column, one address, two deliveries, and the distinction moved inside it. The
              eyebrow names the CHANNEL rather than a moment, because the column spans two moments;
              the address is read back once, at the top, where it serves both; and each delivery is
              one short line naming what it is and when. The load-bearing half is "nothing to
              press": a skimmer must not come away thinking the button is what makes the published
              report arrive, or that not pressing it costs them the report.

              The grid takes one track per VISIBLE column (`grid-auto-flow: column`), so the
              conditional partner column cannot leave a hole and no rule has to be kept in step with
              how many columns there happen to be.
            */}
            <div class="rep-clocks">
              {/*
                ── THE FIRST THING THAT HAPPENS NEXT IS READING THE REST OF THIS PAGE ─────────────

                Dan, 2026-08-28: *"what happens next — maybe we add another one, like 'review your
                report', and it links down into this section."* The section he was scrolling through
                while he said it is 02 onward: the summary, the six scored practices, the coverage
                and what to fix first.

                It leads the row because it is the only entry that costs nothing and is already
                paid for. Everything else here is an offer — watch this, wait for this, join this.
                This one is the thing the reader came for, and until now the page's answer to "what
                happens next" opened by talking about Baseout.

                IT IS CALLED AN ANALYSIS, NOT A REPORT, and that is the same conversation: *"maybe
                we don't call this a report; the report is more the industry overall one."* See
                `lib/brand.ts`. The word "report" on this page now means one document only, the
                published one, and this column is the other thing.

                HIDDEN UNTIL THERE IS SOMETHING TO JUMP TO, AND REVEALED BY ITS OWN TARGET. A link
                that scrolls to a `[hidden]` element does nothing at all and says nothing about it,
                which is the worst version of a broken link. Section 02 is revealed only when a
                stored result actually produced a summary, so this column is revealed on that same
                line rather than beside the verdict: tied to the target, the two cannot get out of
                step, and there is no state where the card is offering a jump into nothing.

                No `scroll-behavior` is set here: `survey.css` already declares smooth scrolling
                inside a `no-preference` block, so this follows the same setting as the rest of the
                app and stops moving when the reader has asked for less motion.
              */}
              <div class="rep-clock" id="rep-jump" hidden>
                <p class="bo-eyebrow rep-clock__when">On this page</p>
                <h3 class="rep-clock__h">Your <ScoreName /> analysis</h3>
                <p class="rep-clock__line">
                  What the number means, the six practices behind it, and what to fix first.
                </p>
                <a class="bo-btn bo-btn--ghost rep-clock__cta" href="#rep-short-sec">
                  Read the analysis <span aria-hidden="true">&darr;</span>
                </a>
              </div>

              <div class="rep-clock">
                <p class="bo-eyebrow rep-clock__when">Now</p>
                <h3 class="rep-clock__h">A first look at Baseout</h3>
                <p class="rep-clock__line">
                  Backup, restore and data intelligence for Airtable, from the team that built
                  On2Air.
                </p>
                <button class="video rep-clock__video" type="button" data-video aria-haspopup="dialog">
                  <div class="video-placeholder">
                    <span aria-hidden="true">&#9654;</span>
                    <p>Preview video coming right here</p>
                  </div>
                </button>
              </div>

              <div class="rep-clock">
                <p class="bo-eyebrow rep-clock__when">By email</p>
                {/* THE HEADING ANSWERS DAN'S QUESTION BEFORE HE ASKS IT AGAIN. He read two inbox
                    columns as two requests for an address; the first thing this column says is that
                    there is one address and two things coming to it. */}
                <h3 class="rep-clock__h">Two things, one address</h3>
                {/* THE ADDRESS, ALONE ON ITS OWN LINE, because it is what the column is about and
                    it now serves BOTH deliveries — which is the whole reason the two columns became
                    one. "Going to … — the address you gave in the survey" was here and went: they
                    know where they typed it. */}
                <p class="rep-clock__to" id="rep-mail-to" hidden>
                  <strong id="rep-mail"></strong>
                </p>

                {/* TWO DELIVERIES, TWO SHORT LINES, ONE BUTTON BETWEEN THEM.

                    This ran to about ten lines while its neighbours ran to two, and Dan said so:
                    *"way too much text and hard to read."* A row of three columns is read ACROSS,
                    and a column three times the length of the ones beside it stops being a column
                    and becomes a paragraph with two captions next to it.

                    What came out was every clause that explained something already on screen: what
                    is in "this page" (the reader is standing on it), where they typed their address,
                    and what the published report contains (promised twice elsewhere on this page and
                    again inside the email). The distinction that mattered survives in the nouns and
                    the timings — YOUR SCORE / NOW against THE FULL REPORT / WHEN IT PUBLISHES — and
                    "nothing to press" is the one clause kept for a misreading, because it is the
                    misreading that the whole four-column detour existed to prevent. */}
                <div class="rep-post">
                  <div>
                    <p class="rep-clock__line">Your score &mdash; now, as plain text.</p>
                    {/* Shown by the script only once there is a score, so it cannot be pressed on a
                        page that has nothing to send. The address is prefilled into the
                        confirmation from the stored result and typed when there is none, the same
                        way the founding-partner dialog handles it. */}
                    <p class="rep-send" id="rep-send-wrap" hidden>
                      {/* "EMAIL ME MY REPORT" UNTIL 2026-08-28. It is the score that gets emailed,
                          and this button stood one line above a sentence promising "the full
                          report" — so the column had the word "report" meaning two different
                          documents within an inch of itself, which is exactly the confusion Dan
                          spent half the video untangling: *"maybe we don't call this a report; the
                          report is more the industry overall one."* One noun each, and the
                          ambiguity goes without a word of explanation being added. */}
                      <button class="bo-btn bo-btn--primary" id="rep-send" type="button" data-token={token} aria-haspopup="dialog">
                        Email me my score
                      </button>
                      <span class="bo-fine rep-send__note" id="rep-send-note" role="status" aria-live="polite"></span>
                    </p>
                  </div>

                  <p class="rep-clock__line">
                    {/* "THE FULL REPORT" NAMED NOTHING. Dan, on this column: *"this one is more
                        like, get notified when the full report is completed — the SURVEY report."*
                        With the button beside it now saying "score", "the survey report" is the
                        only other document on the page and the pair reads without a gloss. */}
                    The survey report &mdash; when it publishes.
                    {/* Conditional for the same reason the address line is: it is a claim about a
                        specific address, and a result stored without one (an older shape, or a
                        respondent who never gave one) would have the page promising a list nobody
                        is on. */}
                    <span id="rep-mail-auto" hidden>Nothing to press; you are on the list.</span>
                  </p>
                </div>
              </div>

              {agency && (
                <div class="rep-clock">
                  <p class="bo-eyebrow rep-clock__when">Agencies/Partners</p>
                  <h3 class="rep-clock__h">Founding partner status</h3>
                  <p class="rep-clock__line">
                    You build for clients. Founding partners keep founding-level terms permanently.
                  </p>
                  {/*
                    NOT A ONE-CLICK COMMITMENT ANY MORE. Dan: *"That button just looks dumb, a user
                    can click it by accident and it's not clear what to expect."* It opens the
                    confirmation below instead, which shows the address they go on the list under,
                    lets them fix it, restates what founding status is, and says what happens next.

                    `data-partner` rather than an id: every trigger on the page opens the one
                    dialog, so a second placement costs an attribute. There is exactly one today —
                    the `?v=a` ending had the other, and it went with the ending.
                  */}
                  <button class="bo-btn bo-btn--primary rep-clock__cta" type="button" data-partner aria-haspopup="dialog">
                    Claim founding status
                  </button>
                  <p class="bo-note bo-note--success" id="partner-confirmed" hidden>
                    You're on the founding list, we'll be in touch.
                  </p>
                </div>
              )}
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ── 02 · the confidence gap ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
        <section class="rep-sec" id="rep-short-sec" hidden>
          <span class="rep-sec__n">02</span>
          <div>
            <h2 class="rep-h2">In short</h2>
            <div class="rep-short">
              <div>
                <p class="rep-short__prose" id="rep-summary"></p>
                <p class="rep-short__trace" id="rep-trace" hidden>
                  What to do about that is section 04, below.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class="rep-short__gap" id="rep-gap-sec" hidden>
                <p class="bo-eyebrow rep-short__gaplabel">The confidence gap</p>
                <p class="rep-gap" id="rep-gap"></p>
                <p class="rep-gap__note" id="rep-gap-note"></p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ── 03 · the table ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
             BACK WHERE IT WAS. This block was briefly moved above the summary on 2026-08-28, on a
             reading of Dan's *"I do worry that this being at the bottom gets missed"* that took
             "this" to mean the audit. It cannot have: the table was second of five, and nobody
             calls the second thing on a page the bottom. Oleh identified what Dan was pointing at
             (`what happens next`, which was literally last) and it now leads. So the summary reads
             before the table again, which was the original order and the right one: the sentence
             says what the number means, the six rows say where it came from. -->
        <section class="rep-sec" id="rep-table-sec" hidden>
          <span class="rep-sec__n">03</span>
          <div>
            <div class="rep-h2row">
              <h2 class="rep-h2">What we measured</h2>
              <span class="bo-eyebrow bo-eyebrow--muted">Six practices &middot; 0 to 3 each</span>
            </div>
            <div class="rep-table__head">
              <span class="bo-eyebrow bo-eyebrow--muted">Practice</span>
              <span class="bo-eyebrow bo-eyebrow--muted">What you said</span>
              <span class="bo-eyebrow bo-eyebrow--muted">Score</span>
              <span class="bo-eyebrow bo-eyebrow--muted rep-table__end">Out of 3</span>
            </div>
            <div id="rep-table"></div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ── 04 · coverage ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
        <section class="rep-sec" id="rep-cov-sec" hidden>
          <span class="rep-sec__n">04</span>
          <div>
            <h2 class="rep-h2">What your backups actually hold</h2>
            <p class="rep-sub">
              From what you were confident enough to tick. Anything unticked is not confirmed,
              rather than known to be missing.
            </p>
            <div class="rep-cov">
              <div>
                <p class="bo-eyebrow rep-cov__ok">Captured</p>
                <div class="rep-cov__chips" id="rep-cov-yes"></div>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="bo-eyebrow rep-cov__maybe">Not confirmed</p>
                <div class="rep-cov__chips" id="rep-cov-no"></div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ── 05 · what to fix first ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
        <section class="rep-sec" id="rep-fix-sec" hidden>
          <span class="rep-sec__n">05</span>
          <div>
            <h2 class="rep-h2">What to fix first</h2>
            <div id="rep-fix"></div>
          </div>
        </section>

        {/*
          ── 06 · THE END OF THE DOCUMENT ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

          The page used to stop on the last line of advice and then hand the reader 200px of nothing
          before the footer — 120px of the section's own bottom padding and 80 more of the page's.
          Between sections that space reads as breathing. After the last one it reads as the page
          giving up. Oleh, 2026-08-31: *"this last screen is missing some closing section."*

          WHAT WAS MISSING WAS NOT COMPOSITION, IT WAS THE OTHER HALF OF A PROMISE. The splash opens
          on "Nobody has measured it. Help us build the benchmark." The finish page answered every
          question the respondent had about THEMSELVES — a number, a band, six practices, what to fix
          — and never once said what their fifteen minutes bought the thing they were asked to help
          build. This section is that sentence, and the one action that follows from it.

          IT ASKS FOR A LINK TO BE COPIED, NOT FOR A SHARE. No backend, no new promise to keep, and
          it is the honest ask: the report they were promised is worth exactly as many answers as it
          has, so passing it on is the same transaction continuing rather than an upsell. It is also
          the only thing on this page that helps the reader's own copy of the report get better.

          ── THE SHAPE, AND THE ONE THING THAT WAS WRONG WITH IT ──────────────────────────────────
          Drawn as four directions and chosen off the canvas: centred, opening on a short vertical
          tail, which is the same way the splash's explainer ends. The rhyme is deliberate — the
          reader met that figure on the way in and meets its echo on the way out.

          The heading was a soft-primary PLATE in the drawing, echoing `.dia__term` literally. Oleh
          killed it and was right: on the splash that lozenge is a node in a figure with a line
          running into it, and here, standing alone over a paragraph, a filled and bordered box at
          an 8px radius is the vocabulary of a BUTTON. Second time this week a shape has read as a
          control — the `+` chip on the merge node was the first. The rule that keeps emerging: a
          fill plus a border plus a radius says "press me" no matter what the words are.

          So it is a heading, set like every other section heading on the page and centred. The tail
          above it does the work the plate was doing, and it cannot be mistaken for anything.
        */}
        <section class="rep-sec rep-sec--end" id="rep-end-sec" hidden>
          <span class="rep-sec__n">06</span>
          <div class="rep-end">
            <span class="rep-end__tail" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <h2 class="rep-h2 rep-end__h">You are in the first one</h2>
            <p class="rep-end__body">
              Nobody had measured how teams protect the bases their business runs on. Now someone
              has, and your answers are in it. The report gets sharper with every team that answers.
            </p>
            {/*
              ── A FIELD AND A BUTTON, WHICH IS THE SHAPE PEOPLE ALREADY KNOW ────────────────────
              Oleh, 2026-08-31: *"let's replace this thing with an input plus a Copy the Link
              button. I think that will be a bit better. And in that input let it write the address
              of this new deploy we made."*

              He is right, and it is worth saying why the lone button was weaker: a button that
              copies something invisible asks the reader to trust that the right thing went to the
              clipboard. The pair shows the value first and acts on it second, which is the share
              pattern every tool uses because it is the one that can be verified before it is used.

              IT IS A REAL `<input readonly>`, not a styled `<span>`. Three things come free and all
              three matter here: a long-press selects it on a phone, `select()` is the whole
              clipboard fallback rather than a `Range` dance, and a screen reader announces a field
              with a value instead of a run of text. `readonly` and not `disabled` — disabled fields
              are skipped by the keyboard and unselectable, which would remove the manual path this
              exists to protect.

              THE ADDRESS COMES FROM `location`, still. It resolves to whatever origin is serving
              the page, so it is right on the deploy, on a preview and on localhost. Today is the
              argument for it: `survey.baseout.dev` stopped resolving because the domain moved to
              `survey.ui.baseout.dev`, and a hardcoded constant would have been quietly handing out
              a dead address from the moment that landed.
            */}
            <p class="rep-end__act">
              <label class="bo-sr" for="rep-url">Link to the survey</label>
              <input
                class="bo-field rep-end__url"
                id="rep-url"
                type="text"
                readonly
                value=""
              />
              {/* BOTH WORDS ARE IN THE BUTTON AT ONCE, stacked in one grid cell. Oleh: *"and the
                  button size must not change."* Swapping the text alone would resize it — "Copied"
                  is wider than "Copy" — and a `min-width` in px is a magic number that goes wrong at
                  a different zoom or font fallback. Two children in the same cell make the button
                  as wide as the WIDER of them, measured by the browser, and it never moves again.

                  `visibility` and not `display`, so the resting word keeps its cell (that is what
                  holds the width) and still leaves the accessibility tree — otherwise the button
                  would be announced as "Copy Copied". */}
              <button class="bo-btn bo-btn--ghost rep-end__copy" id="rep-copy" type="button" data-state="idle">
                <span data-label="idle">Copy</span>
                <span data-label="done">Copied</span>
              </button>
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>
      </article>
    )}
    {!screenedOut && (
      /*
        ONE LIGHTBOX, ONE TRIGGER TODAY AND ROOM FOR MORE. Every `[data-video]` opens it, so a
        second place to play the preview costs an attribute rather than a second dialog to keep in
        step with this one.

        `<dialog>` rather than a div: `showModal()` brings the focus trap, the inert background,
        Escape, and the return of focus to the button that opened it — four behaviours that are
        tedious and easy to get wrong by hand, and all of them free here.
      */
      <dialog class="lightbox" id="video-lightbox" aria-label="A first look at Baseout">
        <button class="lightbox__close" id="video-close" type="button" aria-label="Close the video">
          <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true">
            <line x1="6" y1="6" x2="18" y2="18"></line>
            <line x1="18" y1="6" x2="6" y2="18"></line>
          </svg>
        </button>
        <div class="lightbox__frame">
          {/* TODO(survey-app task 2.3): the unlisted embed goes here. Until it exists the dialog
              shows the same placeholder the page does, at the size the real thing will be. */}
          <div class="video-placeholder">
            <span aria-hidden="true">&#9654;</span>
            <p>Preview video coming right here</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </dialog>
    )}

    {!screenedOut && (
      /*
        THE "EMAIL ME MY REPORT" CONFIRMATION.

        Oleh, 2026-08-28: *"when the user clicks 'Email me my report' they should also see a modal
        that asks again: do you agree that the report will come to you, yes or no?"* The same
        instrument and the same shape as the founding-partner dialog below, deliberately — two
        confirmations on one page that are built differently read as two applications.

        WHAT IT HAS TO SETTLE IS WHICH REPORT. The column beside this one promises the published
        report; this email is the score, the band and the six practices, and the list below is the
        actual contents of what `lib/email.ts` composes, not a flattering summary of it. The line
        naming the other document is in here as well as on the page, because the dialog is the last
        thing read before the press.

        ON TIMING, WHAT IS TRUE. Oleh guessed "up to an hour". `/api/send-report` calls Resend
        synchronously and the mail is away before the button says "Sent", so an hour is an invented
        delay that this page — whose whole argument is that every number on it traces to something
        real — would be caught in the moment it landed in ten seconds. The friction that does exist
        is stated instead: spam, and an address nothing here can tell them later has bounced.
      */
      <dialog class="pmodal" id="mail-modal" aria-labelledby="mmodal-title">
        <form method="dialog" class="pmodal__form" id="mail-form">
          <p class="bo-eyebrow">By email</p>
          <h2 class="pmodal__h" id="mmodal-title">Send this page to you</h2>

          <p class="pmodal__lede">One plain-text email with what you are reading now:</p>
          <ul class="pmodal__perks">
            <li>Your <ScoreName /> out of 100, and the band it falls in</li>
            <li>All six practices, scored 0 to 3, with what you answered to each</li>
            <li>What to fix first, weakest practice first</li>
          </ul>

          <p class="pmodal__lede">
            {REPORT_NAME} is a different document. It publishes later and comes to you on its
            own: pressing this does not bring it any closer, and not pressing it costs you nothing.
          </p>

          <label class="pmodal__label" for="mail-email">The address we will send it to</label>
          <input
            class="bo-field"
            id="mail-email"
            name="email"
            type="email"
            autocomplete="email"
            inputmode="email"
            spellcheck="false"
            placeholder="you@company.com"
          />
          <p class="bo-note bo-note--error pmodal__err" id="mail-error" hidden></p>

          <p class="bo-fine pmodal__next">
            It sends the moment you confirm, so the address has to be right: nothing on this page
            can tell you later that it bounced. If it is not in your inbox, look in spam. Your
            analysis stays on this page either way.
          </p>

          <div class="pmodal__actions">
            <button class="bo-btn bo-btn--ghost" id="mail-cancel" type="button" value="cancel">
              Cancel
            </button>
            <button class="bo-btn bo-btn--primary" id="mail-go" type="button" data-token={token}>
              Confirm
            </button>
          </div>
        </form>
      </dialog>
    )}

    {agency && !screenedOut && (
      /*
        THE FOUNDING-PARTNER CONFIRMATION.

        Same instrument as the lightbox above, for the same reasons: `showModal()` brings the focus
        trap, the inert background, Escape, and the return of focus to the button that opened it.
        A div with a z-index brings none of those and this dialog asks somebody to commit to
        something, so the one that gets the keyboard right is not optional.

        WHAT IS IN IT IS THE ANSWER TO "IT'S NOT CLEAR WHAT TO EXPECT": the address they go on the
        list under and a field to correct it, what founding status actually is (the same four lines
        the page has always promised — no new promise is invented here), and what happens after
        Confirm. Cancel is a real way out and it is the button that closes on Escape too.
      */
      <dialog class="pmodal" id="partner-modal" aria-labelledby="pmodal-title">
        <form method="dialog" class="pmodal__form" id="partner-form">
          <p class="bo-eyebrow">Agencies/Partners</p>
          <h2 class="pmodal__h" id="pmodal-title">Founding partner status</h2>

          <p class="pmodal__lede">
            You said you build for clients. Founding partners get founding perks:
          </p>
          <ul class="pmodal__perks">
            <li>Founding-partner status and badge, permanently</li>
            <li>Early access for you and your clients</li>
            <li>Direct line to the team building it</li>
            <li>Founding-level program terms</li>
          </ul>

          <label class="pmodal__label" for="partner-email">The address we will add you under</label>
          <input
            class="bo-field"
            id="partner-email"
            name="email"
            type="email"
            autocomplete="email"
            inputmode="email"
            spellcheck="false"
            placeholder="you@agency.com"
          />
          <p class="bo-note bo-note--error pmodal__err" id="partner-error" hidden></p>

          <p class="bo-fine pmodal__next">
            Confirming puts you on the founding list. Nothing is charged and nothing is public: we
            email you from this address when the partner program opens, before it is announced.
          </p>

          <div class="pmodal__actions">
            <button class="bo-btn bo-btn--ghost" id="partner-cancel" type="button" value="cancel">
              Cancel
            </button>
            <button class="bo-btn bo-btn--primary" id="partner-go" type="button" data-token={token}>
              Confirm
            </button>
          </div>
        </form>
      </dialog>
    )}
  </main>
</Layout>

<style>
  /* THE NUMBER IS THE LOUDEST THING ON THE PAGE, because it is the thing Dan asked people to come
     for. The denominator is set small and muted beside it: "/ 100" is a unit, not a second number,
     and matching their sizes would make the score read as a fraction to be worked out. */
  .finish-number {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--bo-space-2);
    margin: 0 0 var(--bo-space-1);
    font-size: clamp(3.5rem, 8vw, 5.5rem);
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1;
    letter-spacing: -0.04em;
    color: var(--bo-primary);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .finish-number__of {
    font-size: var(--bo-t-20);
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0;
    color: var(--bo-muted);
  }

  /* The comparison sits under the blurb and above the scale: you read what you are, then how common
     that is, then where it falls on the range. */
  .verdict-compare {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--bo-space-1);
    margin: var(--bo-space-2) 0 0;
    max-width: 34rem;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-16);
    color: var(--bo-base-content);
  }

  /* The video well is a SURFACE, not a picture: base-200 with a base-300 hairline, so it tracks the
     theme instead of staying a near-black rectangle on a dark page.

     It is a BUTTON now, so the browser's own defaults have to be undone before the surface shows:
     a button brings its own font, padding, border and background, and each one of those would
     otherwise fight a rule below. */
  .video {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    padding: 0;
    font: inherit;
    color: inherit;
    background: none;
    cursor: pointer;
    border: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
    border-radius: var(--bo-r-12);
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .video-placeholder {
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    align-content: center;
    gap: 8px;
    background: var(--bo-base-200);
    color: var(--bo-muted);
  }

  .video-placeholder span { font-size: var(--bo-t-32); line-height: 1; }
  .video-placeholder p { margin: 0; font-size: var(--bo-t-14); }

  /* The one hover the page has: the well lifts its hairline and its glyph, so the still rectangle
     says it can be opened without a caption saying so. */
  .video:hover { border-color: var(--bo-muted); }
  .video:hover .video-placeholder { color: var(--bo-base-content); }

  /* ── TAKE IT WITH YOU ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     A COLUMN, NOT A LINE. This used to sit under the score with its status text beside it, where
     there was a full measure to wrap in. In 01 it is one of three narrow columns, and a button
     with a sentence next to it at 260px is a button with one word next to it: the note goes under
     the button and both start at the left edge of the column. `margin-top` matches the founding
     column's CTA, so the two actions in the row sit on the same line as each other. */
  .rep-send {
    display: grid;
    justify-items: start;
    gap: var(--bo-space-2);
    margin: var(--bo-space-1) 0 0;
  }

  /* The note is a status line, not a caption: it keeps the ink of ordinary text when it is
     reporting success and takes the error colour when it is reporting a failure, because a failure
     the reader has to act on cannot be set in the same grey as "sent". */
  .rep-send__note { display: none; }
  .rep-send__note[data-state] { display: block; }
  .rep-send__note[data-state='sent'] { color: var(--bo-success); }
  .rep-send__note[data-state='failed'] { color: var(--bo-error); }

  /* ── THE FOUNDING-PARTNER CONFIRMATION ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     A panel, not a screen: it is one decision with one address on it, so it is capped at a reading
     measure and centred by the dialog element's own margin rather than by a transform. The backdrop
     matches the lightbox's, because two modals on one page that dim the page differently read as
     two different applications. */
  .pmodal {
    width: min(32rem, 92vw);
    max-width: none;
    max-height: 92vh;
    padding: var(--bo-space-4);
    border: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
    border-radius: var(--bo-r-12);
    background: var(--bo-base-100);
    color: var(--bo-base-content);
  }

  .pmodal::backdrop {
    background: oklch(from #000 l c h / 0.72);
    backdrop-filter: blur(2px);
  }

  .pmodal__form {
    display: block;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .pmodal__h {
    margin: var(--bo-space-1) 0 var(--bo-space-2);
    font-size: var(--bo-t-24);
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
  }

  .pmodal__lede {
    margin: 0 0 var(--bo-space-2);
    font-size: var(--bo-t-14);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--bo-muted-strong);
  }

  .pmodal__perks {
    margin: 0 0 var(--bo-space-3);
    padding-left: 20px;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-14);
    color: var(--bo-muted-strong);
  }

  .pmodal__perks li { margin-bottom: 4px; }

  .pmodal__label {
    display: block;
    margin-bottom: var(--bo-space-1);
    font-size: var(--bo-t-13);
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .pmodal__err { margin-top: var(--bo-space-2); }

  .pmodal__next {
    display: block;
    margin: var(--bo-space-2) 0 0;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  /* CONFIRM ON THE RIGHT, CANCEL BESIDE IT. Cancel is a button rather than the dialog's own close
     affordance so that the way out is visible: Escape already closes this, and a way out only
     somebody who knows the platform can find is not a way out. */
  .pmodal__actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: flex-end;
    gap: var(--bo-space-2);
    margin-top: var(--bo-space-4);
  }

  /* ── THE LIGHTBOX ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Centred by the dialog element's own margin rather than by a transform, so nothing has to be
     told the size of the thing it is centring. Sized to the viewport in both directions: a 16:9
     frame capped only by width overflows a laptop in landscape, which is the shape most of them
     are. */
  .lightbox {
    width: min(76rem, 92vw);
    max-width: none;
    max-height: 92vh;
    padding: 0;
    border: 0;
    border-radius: var(--bo-r-12);
    background: none;
    overflow: visible;
    color: var(--bo-base-content);
  }

  .lightbox::backdrop {
    background: oklch(from #000 l c h / 0.72);
    backdrop-filter: blur(2px);
  }

  .lightbox__frame {
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
    max-height: 92vh;
    margin-inline: auto;
    overflow: hidden;
    background: var(--bo-base-200);
    border: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
    border-radius: var(--bo-r-12);
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    align-content: center;
    gap: 8px;
  }

  .lightbox__frame .video-placeholder { border: 0; background: none; }

  /* OUTSIDE THE FRAME AT FULL SIZE, INSIDE IT WHEN THERE IS NO ROOM. Sitting over the video is
     where a close control covers content; sitting outside it is where it disappears on a narrow
     screen. Neither is right at both widths, so it moves. */
  .lightbox__close {
    position: absolute;
    top: -2.75rem;
    right: 0;
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    width: 2.25rem;
    height: 2.25rem;
    padding: 0;
    border: 0;
    border-radius: var(--bo-r-8);
    background: none;
    color: var(--bo-muted);
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  .lightbox__close:hover { color: var(--bo-base-content); }

  @media (max-width: 48rem) {
    .lightbox__close {
      top: 0.5rem;
      right: 0.5rem;
      background: var(--bo-base-100);
    }
  }

  /* ══ THE ISSUED REPORT ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     A document rather than a screen: a masthead that says what this is and when it was issued,
     then numbered sections down one measure. The vocabulary is the app's own — same type ladder,
     same hairline, same accent — so it reads as the survey's last page and not as a second brand. */
  .rep-mast {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: var(--bo-space-3);
    padding-bottom: var(--bo-space-2);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
    font-size: var(--bo-t-11);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .rep-mast__mark { color: var(--bo-primary); }
  .rep-mast__meta { color: var(--bo-muted); font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }

  .rep-h1 {
    margin: var(--bo-space-1) 0 var(--bo-space-2);
    font-size: var(--bo-t-32);
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.15;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }

  /* THE THANK-YOU AND THE RESULT ARE ONE SCREEN, THE SECTIONS ARE SLIDES.
     Oleh, 2026-08-27: the message and the score should land together, and everything after them
     should be far enough apart to be scrolled through like a presentation — which is also the
     structure a scroll-driven animation will need, so the spacing is where that starts.

     So the verdict closes UP against the opening (a rhythm between them, not three) and the
     numbered sections open OUT. Nothing else about them changes. */
  .rep-verdict {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.15fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--bo-space-6);
    align-items: start;
    padding: var(--bo-space-3) 0 var(--bo-space-5);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
  }

  .rep-number { margin-bottom: var(--bo-space-2); }

  .rep-band {
    margin: 0 0 var(--bo-space-2);
    font-size: var(--bo-t-display-sm);
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.05;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
  }

  /* The sitting itself: how much they gave, which a grade never says back to them. */
  .rep-facts {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: var(--bo-space-3);
    padding-top: var(--bo-space-3);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
  }

  .rep-facts__n {
    display: block;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-32);
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .rep-facts__l {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 6px;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-11);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--bo-muted);
  }

  /* FIVE RHYTHMS TOP AND BOTTOM, not two. That is 120px each side, so two sections' content sits
     240px apart and each one arrives on its own rather than as the next paragraph of the last.

     It stays on the 24px rhythm the rest of this app is built on, and it is a single custom
     property because the scroll-snap step will want to read it: whoever adds `scroll-snap-align`
     to these sections should change `--rep-slide` and nothing else. */
  .rep { --rep-slide: calc(var(--bo-rhythm) * 5); }

  /* ── A NUMBERED SECTION · THE NUMERAL ON THE RULE ────────────────────────────────────────────
     No numeral column, so every section runs the full measure. `position: relative` is here for
     the numeral, which is lifted onto the hairline above it and given the page's own floor behind
     it, so the rule appears to break around it rather than to run behind it. `--bo-root-bg` is the
     floor `body` actually paints, not `--bo-base-100` — a panel colour would show as a lighter
     notch in the rule.

     THESE THREE DECLARATIONS USED TO SIT ON `.rep`, one specificity step
     above the `@media (max-width: 62rem)` rule below that re-declares `grid-template-columns` and
     `gap` for the same element. That media rule never had a say and still does not — folding the
     treatment down to `.rep-sec` would have handed it one, and changed the layout under 62rem, so
     its two dead declarations came out in the same edit. */
  /* `scroll-margin-top` IS NOT COSMETIC HERE. The section's numeral sits ON its top border, half
     above the line and half below, so an anchor that lands the border-box at y=0 cuts the numeral
     in two against the top of the window — measured, not guessed, the first time `#rep-jump`'s link
     was pressed. One rhythm of margin clears it and lands the section with the mark intact. It is
     declared on every section rather than on the one that is linked today, because the next anchor
     added to this page would otherwise arrive with the same bug and no reason to expect it. */
  .rep-sec {
    position: relative;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 0;
    padding: var(--rep-slide) 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
    scroll-margin-top: var(--bo-rhythm);
  }

  .rep-sec:last-child { border-bottom: 0; }

  /* INSET, SO THE RULE RUNS EITHER SIDE OF IT. Flush left the numeral sat at the start of the
     hairline and read as a tab on it rather than a mark cut into it — the rule was truncated, not
     interrupted. A rhythm of inset leaves a stub of rule to its left, which is what makes it read
     as sitting ON the line. Padding both sides for the same reason. */
  .rep-sec__n {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    left: var(--bo-space-3);
    transform: translateY(-50%);
    padding: 0 var(--bo-space-2);
    background: var(--bo-root-bg);
    line-height: 1;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-13);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    color: var(--bo-primary);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .rep-h2 {
    margin: 0 0 var(--bo-space-3);
    font-size: var(--bo-t-24);
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
  }

  .rep-h2row {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: var(--bo-space-3);
    margin-bottom: var(--bo-space-3);
  }

  .rep-h2row .rep-h2 { margin: 0; }

  /* A heading directly followed by its own explanatory line closes up: the pair is one unit, and
     the section's own air is what separates it from what comes next. */
  .rep-h2:has(+ .rep-sub) { margin-bottom: var(--bo-space-1); }

  .rep-sub {
    margin: 0 0 var(--bo-space-3);
    max-width: 42rem;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-14);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--bo-muted);
  }

  .rep-gap {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 42rem;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-18);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--bo-base-content);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  .rep-gap__note {
    margin: var(--bo-space-2) 0 0;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-14);
    color: var(--bo-muted);
  }

  .rep-table__head {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 200px) minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 180px) 76px;
    gap: 0 var(--bo-space-3);
    align-items: baseline;
    padding-bottom: var(--bo-space-1);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
  }

  .rep-table__end { text-align: right; }

  .rep-cov {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: var(--bo-space-5);
  }

  .rep-cov__ok { color: var(--bo-success); }
  .rep-cov__maybe { color: var(--bo-warning); }

  .rep-cov__chips {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--bo-space-1);
    margin-top: var(--bo-space-2);
  }

  /* ── 06 · THE CLOSING SECTION ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Centred, which is the only centred block on a page that is otherwise set left. That is the
     point: the document has ended, and the change of axis says so before a word is read.

     LESS BOTTOM PADDING THAN ITS SIBLINGS. `--rep-slide` gives every section 120px on each side,
     which is right BETWEEN sections and wrong after the last one — under it there is only the
     footer, and the reader was already looking at 200px of nothing. The top keeps its full slide;
     the bottom takes half. */
  .rep-sec--end {
    padding-bottom: calc(var(--rep-slide) / 2);
  }

  .rep-end {
    display: grid;
    justify-items: center;
    text-align: center;
    gap: var(--bo-space-3);
  }

  /* The same gesture the splash's explainer ends on: a short drop into the conclusion. It is a
     `<span>` with a background rather than a border on the heading, so it keeps its own space in
     the grid's `gap` rhythm and nothing has to be nudged around it.

     TWO LAYERS, LIKE THE EXPLAINER. The element is the TRACK, neutral and always drawn; `::after`
     is the LIT PASS, accent, and it is the only thing that moves. That is the same construction as
     `.dia__base` under `.dia__on` on the splash — Oleh asked for this line to animate *"like the
     diagram on the home page, so a blue pulse comes down from the top to the words"*.

     THE LIT LAYER RESTS INVISIBLE, and it is declared here rather than inside the motion block on
     purpose. With reduced motion the animation is never created, so whatever is declared HERE is
     what a reader sees — a neutral line and nothing else, which is the honest still frame of a
     gesture whose whole content is the movement. Compare the splash's explainer, which pins its
     final lit state instead: there the accent path is information (it shows which way the flow
     runs) and here it is punctuation. */
  .rep-end__tail {
    position: relative;
    width: 1px;
    height: calc(var(--bo-rhythm) * 2.5);
    background: var(--bo-base-300);
  }

  /* THE BEAM. It is a SHORT SEGMENT that travels the length of the track, not a fill that grows
     down it — Oleh, 2026-08-31: *"as soon as the blue line arrives it sort of lights up the words
     underneath it. Then everything goes white again. Like a beam moving from top to bottom, and as
     soon as it reaches the end the words go blue."*

     The first build grew the line from 0 to full height and left it drawn, which is a PROGRESS BAR:
     it says how far along something is. A beam says something travelled. They look similar for the
     first frame and mean opposite things, and what he is after is the second.

     40% of the track, with the accent fading out at both ends, so it has no hard edges to catch on
     — a segment with square ends reads as a block sliding, and a gradient reads as light. It rests
     parked above the track at zero opacity, which is also the whole reduced-motion state. */
  .rep-end__tail::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    height: 40%;
    background: linear-gradient(
      to bottom,
      transparent 0%,
      var(--bo-primary) 40%,
      var(--bo-primary) 60%,
      transparent 100%
    );
    transform: translateY(-100%);
    opacity: 0;
  }

  /* IT IS A HEADING, NOT A PLATE. The drawing had this as a soft-primary lozenge echoing the
     explainer's terminal node, and Oleh cut it: in the figure that shape is a node with a line
     running into it, and standing alone above a paragraph a fill plus a border plus an 8px radius
     is the vocabulary of a button. `.rep-h2` carries the size and weight; only the margin changes,
     because the grid's `gap` owns the spacing here. */
  /* ACCENT, on Oleh's note — *"and the heading can probably be blue"*. It is the one heading on
     this page that is not ink, and that is the point: it is not a section of the report, it is what
     the report was for. The pulse below runs down into it, so the line and the words it lands on
     are the same colour and read as one gesture rather than as a decoration beside a title. */
  .rep-end__h {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--bo-primary);
  }

  .rep-end__body {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 38rem;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-16);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--bo-muted-strong);
  }

  /* The field and the button that acts on it, on one line. `margin: 0` because the section's grid
     owns the spacing between rows.

     IT WRAPS RATHER THAN SHRINKS. Below about 30rem the field and the button cannot both hold their
     minimums on one line, and a squeezed field showing half a hostname is worse than two rows. On
     the wrap the button takes the full width under the field, which is also the bigger target — see
     the touch pass: this is the last control on the page and the one most likely to be pressed with
     a thumb. */
  .rep-end__act {
    display: flex;
    /* STRETCH, NOT CENTRE. `.bo-field` renders 49px here and `.bo-btn` 45 — their paddings and
       line-heights land differently on the same 2.75rem floor — and 4px of mismatch between two
       boxes sitting on one line is exactly the kind of thing that reads as sloppy without being
       nameable. Stretching lets the row settle on one height and takes the button up with it, which
       also makes the target bigger rather than smaller. */
    align-items: stretch;
    /* One rung tighter than the section's other gaps. The field and the button are ONE control in
       two parts, and a rhythm's worth of air between them reads as two separate offers. */
    gap: var(--bo-space-1);
    margin: 0;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: center;
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 30rem;
  }

