    gap: var(--bo-space-5);
    align-items: start;
  }

  .rep-short__prose {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-18);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--bo-base-content);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  /* The one line that ties a problem to where it gets solved. Oleh asked for exactly this: the
     summary should not name a failure and leave the reader hunting for what to do about it. */
  .rep-short__trace {
    margin: var(--bo-space-2) 0 0;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-14);
    color: var(--bo-muted);
  }

  .rep-short__gap { border-left: 2px solid var(--bo-primary); padding-left: var(--bo-space-3); }
  .rep-short__gaplabel { margin: 0 0 var(--bo-space-1); color: var(--bo-primary); }

  .rep-gap { font-size: var(--bo-t-14); }
  .rep-gap__note { font-size: var(--bo-t-14); }

  /* ── 01 · THE CLOCKS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     ONE TRACK PER VISIBLE COLUMN. The partner column only exists for agencies, so a fixed track
     count has to be maintained in two places and gets one of them wrong the day a column is added:
     this file carried `repeat(2)` and `repeat(3)` behind `data-agency`, and the email column moved
     that to three and four before the two inbox columns merged back into one. `grid-auto-flow:
     column` counts the children that are actually there — `[hidden]` is `display: none` and lays no
     track — so the layout is right for two or three without anybody being told, and a column added
     or removed later does not need this rule edited at all.

     NO RULE ABOVE THE COLUMNS. There was a `border-top` here and Oleh took it out, correctly:
     *"delete the horizontal line between 'What happens next' and the cards, it just contradicts
     the other lines we have here."* Every other full-measure hairline on this page is a
     `.rep-sec` bottom border and means one section has ended. This one ran the same weight between
     a heading and the thing it heads, where nothing ends, so the page had two identical rules
     saying two different things. The vertical dividers below stay: they separate parallel things,
     which is a different statement and a different axis.

     The air the border used to hold is stated once here, on the rhythm, rather than falling out of
     a border plus each column's own padding. PADDING RATHER THAN MARGIN, and that is the whole
     reason the number is right: a `margin-top` here is an adjacent-sibling margin against the
     `.rep-h2`'s own `space-3` bottom margin, the two collapse to one, and the gap silently halves.
     Padding does not collapse, so heading + grid is `space-3` + `space-3` — two rhythms, which is
     what the border and the column padding used to add up to between them. */
  .rep-clocks {
    display: grid;
    grid-auto-flow: column;
    grid-auto-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    padding-top: var(--bo-space-3);
  }

  /* The columns no longer pad away from a rule above them, so the divider between them starts at
     the top of the first eyebrow rather than in mid-air below a line. */
  .rep-clock {
    display: grid;
    align-content: start;
    gap: var(--bo-space-2);
    padding: 0 var(--bo-space-4) 0 0;
  }

  /* The rule between columns is the only division. No boxes: three cards of equal weight is what
     made these read as interchangeable in the first place. */
  .rep-clock + .rep-clock {
    padding-left: var(--bo-space-4);
    border-left: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
  }

  /* A HIDDEN COLUMN LAYS NO TRACK BUT IS STILL A SIBLING. `+` and `:last-child` are structural:
     they count elements, not boxes, and `[hidden]` only takes the box away. `#rep-jump` leads this
     row and starts hidden, so without this rule the Baseout column — visually the first thing in
     the row — inherits a divider and a gutter for a neighbour that is not being drawn, and the row
     opens on a vertical rule standing in the margin. Only the FIRST column can be hidden today, so
     one guard covers it; a hidden column in the middle would need the pair either side. */
  .rep-clock[hidden] + .rep-clock {
    padding-left: 0;
    border-left: 0;
  }

  .rep-clock:last-child { padding-right: 0; }

  /* THE MIDDLE COLUMN IS THE ONE THAT IS MUTED, and that needed saying the other way round: the
     base `.bo-eyebrow` is ALREADY the accent, so a `--live` modifier painting it accent did nothing
     and all three labels came out the same blue. Two of these are things you can act on now; the
     report is a wait, and it takes `--muted` so the row says so at a glance. */

  .rep-clock__h {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-18);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .rep-clock__line {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-14);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--bo-muted);
  }

  .rep-clock__to {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-14);
    color: var(--bo-base-content);
  }

  /* ── TWO DELIVERIES INSIDE ONE COLUMN ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     One rhythm between them and nothing else: no labels, no rule, no box. The labelled version of
     this was the long one — a second labelling system inside a column that the row already labels,
     and every label needed a sentence under it to earn its place. Two lines of `.rep-clock__line`,
     the same body type the columns beside this one use, with the button belonging to the first of
     them because they share a `<div>` and the gap only falls between the two deliveries. */
  .rep-post {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--bo-space-3);
  }

  .rep-clock__video { margin-top: var(--bo-space-1); }
  .rep-clock__cta { justify-self: start; margin-top: var(--bo-space-1); }

  /* STACKED, THE DIVIDER TURNS WITH THE AXIS. This `border-top` is between two columns, not
     between the heading and the columns, so it is the horizontal twin of the vertical rule above
     and it stays. `grid-auto-flow: row` is what `grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr)` used to
     say, now that the track count is implicit. */
  @media (max-width: 62rem) {
    .rep-clocks {
      grid-auto-flow: row;
      grid-auto-columns: auto;
    }

    /* A RHYTHM ON BOTH SIDES OF THE RULE. Stacked, the divider used to sit hard against the bottom
       of the column above it — invisible while that column ended in a paragraph, obvious now that
       one of them ends in a button. The gap is `space-3` above and below, so the horizontal rule is
       centred in a rhythm the way the vertical ones are centred in the column gutters. */
    .rep-clock { padding: 0; }

    /* `padding-left` HAS TO BE RESET HERE, and it is a bug fix rather than housekeeping. The
       desktop rule that draws the divider between columns also insets them from it, and
       `.rep-clock + .rep-clock` out-specifies the `.rep-clock { padding: 0 }` above — so stacked,
       every card except the first sat 36px further right than the section heading, in a ragged step
       nobody had noticed because on a desktop the same declaration is correct. Measured: card one
       at padding-left 0 and the other three at 36. */
    .rep-clock + .rep-clock {
      padding-left: 0;
      border-left: 0;
      border-top: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
    }

    /* The same guard, turned with the axis: stacked, a hidden first column would leave a rule
       floating above the first column that is actually drawn. */
    .rep-clock[hidden] + .rep-clock {
      padding-top: 0;
      border-top: 0;
    }

    /* ── 01 GETS SMALLER SO THE SEAMS AROUND IT CAN GET BIGGER ────────────────────────────────
       Oleh: *"I think the elements need to be made smaller overall. Right now we say 'what happens
       next' and then in front of the user is a pile of vertical text they have to work out how to
       use. The video preview could be made small. It all needs to be more compact so they see: ah,
       these are my next steps."*

       He is describing the shape of the problem exactly. Stacked, four columns become four full
       sections of prose, each opening with a label and a heading, and the run reads as an article
       rather than as a short list of things a person can do. Measured before this pass: the four
       cards came to 1105px and section 01 to 1264 on an 844px viewport.

       Three cuts, and none of them removes anything a reader needs:

         THE HEADING drops 18px to 16. At column widths those headings sit beside each other and
         carry the row; stacked, each one is alone above its own paragraph and only has to be
         heavier than the body under it.

         THE VIDEO becomes a THUMBNAIL. It keeps 16/9, because that shape is what says "a video goes
         here" and a bar would not, but it stops spanning the column: 12rem wide is 192 x 108, which
         is a preview rather than a player and takes about 50px off the tallest card.

         THE SEAM BETWEEN CARDS halves, 24px a side to 12. Four cards separated by 48px of air plus
         a rule were competing with the 144px that now separates whole SECTIONS — and a boundary
         only reads as a boundary when it is bigger than the ones inside it. */
    .rep-clock:not(:last-child) { padding-bottom: var(--bo-space-2); }
    .rep-clock + .rep-clock { padding-top: var(--bo-space-2); }

    .rep-clock__h {
      font-size: var(--bo-t-16);
    }

    .rep-clock__video {
      max-width: 12rem;
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 62rem) {
    .rep-short {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: var(--bo-space-4);
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 62rem) {
    /* ── MORE AIR BETWEEN SECTIONS, NOT LESS ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       This was `--bo-space-5` on the reasoning that "a slide's worth of air is presentation on a
       desktop and dead scrolling on a phone, where the viewport already separates one section from
       the next". The premise is false on this page and the measurement says so: section 01 is
       1264px tall on an 844px viewport, so the viewport never holds a whole section and never
       separates anything. 48px against 1264 is 3.8% — a boundary that reads as a paragraph break.

       Oleh, 2026-08-31: *"there should be bigger gaps between sections. The analytics sections, the
       what-to-do-next section. Right now it is all in one lump and it is hard to understand what is
       going on."*

       So the seam grows to 72 a side, 144 between sections, WHILE the content inside 01 shrinks —
       see the block below. That pairing is the whole fix: what makes a boundary read is not its
       absolute size but its size against the rhythm either side of it. Making everything bigger
       would have changed nothing. */
    .rep { --rep-slide: var(--bo-space-6); }

    .rep-verdict,
    .rep-cov {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: var(--bo-space-4);
      align-items: start;
    }
  }
</style>

