     ordinary ink also gives the accent a single job on this page: it is the number. The other three
     bands keep their colour because none of them collides. */
  #result-band[data-band='Managed'] { color: var(--bo-base-content); }

  /* ── SCROLL SNAP IS OFF, AND IT IS NOT COMING BACK IN THIS SHAPE ─────────────────────────────
     The brief (`research/survey-app/animation-brief.md`) asks for "a light scroll settles on the
     next section". Both attempts at it — `scroll-snap-align: center` and `start` — made the page
     worse in the same way, and Oleh hit it immediately: the scroll grabs, hangs on a section, and
     fights anyone trying to get past it.

     THE CAUSE IS ARITHMETIC, NOT TUNING. A snap area taller than the scrollport cannot satisfy its
     alignment, so the browser keeps pulling back to the edge it can reach. Measured on an 844px
     viewport: `rep-table-sec` is 1009px and `rep-next-sec` is 976px. Two of the five sections are
     traps by construction, and `center` is worse than `start` because neither edge is reachable.

     Proximity does not save it either: it changes WHEN the snap engages, never whether the area it
     engages on is satisfiable.

     So the reveals stay and the snap goes. Getting the effect Oleh asked for needs every section to
     FIT the viewport — that is a content decision, not a CSS one, and it is his call rather than
     something to fake with a scroll handler. Hijacking the wheel to animate between sections is the
     other way people do this, and it is worse: it takes the page away from anyone using a keyboard,
     a trackpad with momentum, or a screen reader. */

  @media (max-width: 48rem) {
    .rep-row,
    .rep-fix {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: 8px;
    }

    .rep-row__score,
    .rep-fix__where { text-align: left; }

    /* THE HEAD HAS TO COLLAPSE WITH ITS ROWS. It was left out of this block, so below 48rem it kept
       four tracks while the rows beneath it stacked into one: at 390px the labels overlapped into
       "WHASTCORE" and stopped labelling anything. A column head only means something while it sits
       above the column it names, so once the columns are gone it goes too. */
    .rep-table__head { display: none; }
  }

  /* ══ MOTION ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     `research/survey-app/animation-brief.md` is the spec.

     THREE THINGS THIS BLOCK IS ARRANGED AROUND.

     · THE RESTING STATE IN THE MARKUP IS THE FINISHED ONE. Every hidden start state below sits
       behind `[data-anim='on']`, and that attribute is stamped from script. A reader whose
       JavaScript never arrives is served the whole report rather than an empty page waiting for an
       observer that will not run.
     · REDUCED MOTION MEANS NONE OF IT, and it is said twice. The whole block is inside
       `prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference`, and the script separately refuses to stamp the
       attribute or build the observer. Either guard alone would hold; the point is that neither is
       the only one.
     · NOTHING HERE MOVES A LAYOUT BOX. `opacity` and `transform` do all of it, including the two
       places where not doing it would have been easier: the ladder fill SCALES rather than growing
       its `width`, and the needle TRANSLATES rather than shifting its `left`.

     THE STAGGER IS ARITHMETIC IN CSS, NOT A LOOP IN JS. Every revealed element resolves one
     expression — `(--base + --n) × --rep-step + --after` — and each term is set by an ordinary
     selector: `--n` by `:nth-child()`, `--after` by the two things that must follow their own
     neighbour, `--base` by the single ordering CSS cannot count for itself. So the script marks
     five sections and nothing else; it never walks a list of children.

     WHY THIS IS IN THE GLOBAL BLOCK. The table rows, the coverage chips and the advice rows are
     built with `createElement`, so they never carry Astro's scope attribute and a rule in the
     scoped block cannot see them. Three separate defects have been filed against that. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    /* ── THE CLOCKS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       (This comment used to describe a `scroll-snap-align` rule that was taken out with the snap —
       see the note above the responsive block — and nothing replaced the text, so it sat over the
       timing variables describing something that had not been here for two edits.) */

    .rep[data-anim='on'] {
      --rep-in: 520ms;
      --rep-step: 60ms;
      /* Ease-out quint. Decelerates onto its mark; no overshoot, which on a page reporting a
         measurement would read as the measurement wobbling. */
      --rep-ease: cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1);
    }

    /* THE WORDS COME AFTER THE NUMBER, which is the whole argument for animating this at all: the
       reader is meant to read 67 and then read what 67 means. 1080ms is the count-up's 1100 less a
       frame, so the band name is already moving as the last digit settles. Keyframes rather than a
       transition because there is no state change to hang a transition on: this fires on load. */
    .rep[data-anim='on'] .rep-band,
    .rep[data-anim='on'] .bo-verdict__blurb,
    .rep[data-anim='on'] .verdict-compare {
      animation: rep-rule-arrive var(--rep-in) var(--rep-ease) both;
      animation-delay: calc(1080ms + var(--n, 0) * 110ms);
    }

    .rep[data-anim='on'] .bo-verdict__blurb { --n: 1; }
    .rep[data-anim='on'] .verdict-compare { --n: 2; }

    @keyframes rep-rule-arrive {
      from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(8px); }
      to { opacity: 1; transform: none; }
    }

    /* ── 3 · THE SECTION REVEALS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       ONE LIST, TWO RULES: what is hidden, and what it becomes once its own section has been
       marked. The `:is()` is kept free of ids so its specificity stays at a single class and the
       `--after` overrides below can be plain selectors rather than an escalation.

       THE HEADINGS ARE NOT IN THIS LIST, ON PURPOSE. `.rep-h2` is the thing the reader's eye lands
       on as the section arrives, and the brief's own "not wanted" list ends with motion on
       anything the reader is trying to read at that moment. The heading holds still and the
       content arrives against it. */
    .rep[data-anim='on'] :is(
      .rep-short__prose,
      .rep-short__trace,
      .rep-table__head,
      .rep-row,
      .rep-cov__ok,
      .rep-cov__maybe,
      .rep-chip,
      .rep-fix > *,
      .rep-clock
    ) {
      opacity: 0;
      transform: translateY(10px);
    }

    .rep[data-anim='on'] .rep-sec[data-arrived='true'] :is(
      .rep-short__prose,
      .rep-short__trace,
      .rep-table__head,
      .rep-row,
      .rep-cov__ok,
      .rep-cov__maybe,
      .rep-chip,
      .rep-fix > *,
      .rep-clock
    ) {
      opacity: 1;
      transform: none;
      transition:
        opacity var(--rep-in) var(--rep-ease),
        transform var(--rep-in) var(--rep-ease);
      transition-delay: calc((var(--base, 0) + var(--n, 0)) * var(--rep-step) + var(--after, 0ms));
    }

    /* ── 06 · THE PULSE, AND THE WORDS IT LANDS ON ─────────────────────────────────────────────
       The line draws top to bottom, the heading turns from ink to accent as it arrives, the line
       dissolves, the heading fades back, and it all goes round again. Oleh, 2026-08-31: *"let the
       animation keep going, cyclically. And maybe the line runs, the text is white, and then when
       it reaches the text the text goes blue — slowly, where the line reaches it, to continue that
       vertical effect."*

       ONE CLOCK, TWO ANIMATIONS, NO DELAYS. Both are 6s and each encodes its own window in its own
       keyframes — the same construction as the splash's explainer, and for the same reason its
       comment gives: equal periods plus different `animation-delay` values drift apart on some
       engines, one shared period cannot. 6s is that figure's tempo too, so the two moments on this
       site that use this gesture breathe at the same rate.

       THE SCHEDULE, in seconds of the 6:
         0.0 - 0.4   the beam fades in at the top of the track
         0.0 - 1.2   it travels the length of it and exits the bottom
         0.86- 1.2   the heading lights, opening as the beam's tip lands and closing as it clears
         1.2 - 3.7   the heading holds accent
         3.7 - 5.0   it fades back to ink, alone, nothing else moving
         5.0 - 6.0   rest, then again

       The travel is the fast part and the return is the slow one: a beam that dawdles is not a beam,
       and a colour that snaps back is a blink. The whole point of looping is that the CAUSE stays
       visible — a heading left permanently accent after the first pass would have the beam arriving
       at a conclusion already drawn, on every cycle after the first.

       IT DEPARTS FROM THIS PAGE'S OWN RULE ABOUT HEADINGS, and that is worth being explicit about.
       The reveal block above says headings are excluded on purpose — "motion on anything the reader
       is trying to read". A hue shift is a different thing from that ban: nothing moves, nothing
       changes opacity, the glyphs never leave the place the eye put them, and the type stays over
       4.5:1 against the ground at both ends. Oleh asked for it and gave the reason — the colour is
       what carries the gesture from the line into the words.

       THE INK START LIVES IN HERE, NOT OUTSIDE. `.rep-end__h` is accent at rest, which is the state
       a reduced-motion reader gets and the one Oleh chose. Declaring the ink start inside this
       `no-preference` block means the animated case begins ink from its first paint — so there is
       no frame where an accent heading snaps to ink as the section arrives. */
    .rep[data-anim='on'] .rep-end__h {
      color: var(--bo-base-content);
    }

    .rep[data-anim='on'] .rep-sec[data-arrived='true'] .rep-end__tail::after {
      animation: repTail 6s linear infinite;
    }

    .rep[data-anim='on'] .rep-sec[data-arrived='true'] .rep-end__h {
      animation: repEndInk 6s linear infinite;
    }

    /* `translateY` is a percentage of the BEAM's own height, not the track's: the beam is 40% of a
       60px track, so 250% of 24px carries its top edge to 60px — the bottom of the track — and the
       whole segment is clear of it. Parked at -100% it sits entirely above. Linear timing, because
       a beam that eases is a beam that is braking. */
    @keyframes repTail {
      0% {
        transform: translateY(-100%);
        opacity: 0;
      }
      7% {
        opacity: 1;
      }
      20% {
        transform: translateY(250%);
        opacity: 1;
      }
      24%,
      100% {
        transform: translateY(250%);
        opacity: 0;
      }
    }

    /* IT LIGHTS AS THE BEAM TOUCHES IT, AND THE NUMBER IS DERIVED RATHER THAN PICKED.

       Oleh, on the first pass: *"the gap between the line reaching the bottom and the blue coming
       on feels a bit long. It should be a bit faster."* He is right, and the cause is that 18% was
       measured against the wrong edge of the beam.

       The beam is 24px tall and travels from top=-24 to top=60 over the first 20% of the cycle. Its
       LEADING edge crosses the end of the 60px track when top=36 — which is 60/84 of the way, so
       14.3% of the cycle. The old turn opened at 18%: 220ms after the light had already arrived,
       and 220ms is exactly long enough to read as a wait rather than as a consequence.

       So it opens at 14% — the frame the beam's tip lands — and completes at 20%, the frame the
       beam finishes clearing. 360ms instead of 480, and every one of them spent while the light is
       still crossing the words rather than after it has gone. */
    @keyframes repEndInk {
      0%,
      14% {
        color: var(--bo-base-content);
      }
      20%,
      62% {
        color: var(--bo-primary);
      }
      84%,
      100% {
        color: var(--bo-base-content);
      }
    }

    /* `--n` is the position in the queue. Anything a section opens with sits at 0; the repeated
       children below count from 1, so a column head is always a step ahead of its own first row. */
    .rep[data-anim='on'] :is(.rep-row, .rep-chip, .rep-fix, .rep-clock):nth-child(1) { --n: 1; }
    .rep[data-anim='on'] :is(.rep-row, .rep-chip, .rep-fix, .rep-clock):nth-child(2) { --n: 2; }
    .rep[data-anim='on'] :is(.rep-row, .rep-chip, .rep-fix, .rep-clock):nth-child(3) { --n: 3; }
    .rep[data-anim='on'] :is(.rep-row, .rep-chip, .rep-fix, .rep-clock):nth-child(4) { --n: 4; }
    .rep[data-anim='on'] :is(.rep-row, .rep-chip, .rep-fix, .rep-clock):nth-child(5) { --n: 5; }
    .rep[data-anim='on'] :is(.rep-row, .rep-chip, .rep-fix, .rep-clock):nth-child(6) { --n: 6; }
    .rep[data-anim='on'] :is(.rep-row, .rep-chip, .rep-fix, .rep-clock):nth-child(7) { --n: 7; }

    /* ── 03 · WHAT WE MEASURED ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       Six rows from the top, a step apart, and then each row reads its own score out: the ticks
       light left to right AFTER their row has landed, which is what `--after` is for. This is the
       section the respondent came for, and it is the only place on the page where two clocks are
       nested rather than queued. */
    .rep[data-anim='on'] .rep-table__head { --n: 0; }

    .rep[data-anim='on'] .rep-row__tick[data-on='true'] {
      transform: scaleX(0);
      transform-origin: 0 50%;
    }

    .rep[data-anim='on'] .rep-sec[data-arrived='true'] .rep-row__tick[data-on='true'] {
      transform: none;
      transition: transform 240ms var(--rep-ease);
      /* `--n` is inherited from the row, so a tick knows when its own row landed without anyone
         telling it twice. */
      transition-delay: calc(var(--n, 0) * var(--rep-step) + var(--rep-in) + var(--t, 0) * 90ms);
    }

    .rep[data-anim='on'] .rep-row__tick:nth-child(1) { --t: 0; }
    .rep[data-anim='on'] .rep-row__tick:nth-child(2) { --t: 1; }
    .rep[data-anim='on'] .rep-row__tick:nth-child(3) { --t: 2; }

    /* ── 02 · IN SHORT ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       The prose rises; the line that points at section 04 follows it, because it is a footnote to
       the prose and reads as one only if it arrives as one. */
    .rep[data-anim='on'] .rep-short__prose { --n: 0; }
    .rep[data-anim='on'] .rep-short__trace { --n: 1; }

    /* THE CONFIDENCE GAP IS WIPED IN BY ITS OWN EDGE. The panel's 2px accent border is already the
       leftmost thing in it, so a cover painted the page's floor colour and slid off to the right
       leaves the edge standing first and draws the text out of it — the edge reads as the pen.
       `--bo-root-bg` is what `body` actually paints; a panel colour here would show as a pale
       block over the floor. It is a `transform` rather than the `clip-path` this effect would
       ordinarily be written with, because the brief asks for transform and opacity and nothing
       else. */
    /* `overflow: clip` IS LOAD-BEARING, NOT TIDINESS, AND THAT COST A DEFECT TO LEARN. The cover
       ends its wipe at `translateX(100%)`, which parks a 490px panel-width block entirely outside
       its parent's right edge. A transformed box moves no layout box — and still counts toward
       the SCROLLABLE OVERFLOW AREA. Measured without this line at 1440: `scrollWidth` 1789
       against `clientWidth` 1425, so 364px of horizontal scrollbar and the whole report shoved
       left. "No layout shift" and "no overflow" are not the same claim. `clip` rather than
       `hidden` because
       `hidden` would make the panel a scroll container and a formatting context it has no reason
       to be; `clip` cuts at the padding box, which is exactly the rectangle the cover fills, and
       leaves the accent border it slides out of untouched. */
    .rep[data-anim='on'] .rep-short__gap {
      position: relative;
      overflow: clip;
    }

    .rep[data-anim='on'] .rep-short__gap::after {
      content: '';
      position: absolute;
      inset: 0;
      background: var(--bo-root-bg);
      transform: translateX(0);
      transition: transform 620ms var(--rep-ease);
      transition-delay: calc(var(--rep-step) * 2);
      pointer-events: none;
    }

    .rep[data-anim='on'] .rep-sec[data-arrived='true'] .rep-short__gap::after {
      transform: translateX(100%);
    }

    /* ── 04 · WHAT YOUR BACKUPS HOLD ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       Captured, then not confirmed: "here is what you have" before "here is what you have not
       confirmed", which is the reading order the section is written in. The two columns are
       siblings and CSS cannot count the first one's chips to offset the second, so `--base` is the
       one number the script hands over — set once on the column, inherited by its label and every
       chip under it. */
    .rep[data-anim='on'] :is(.rep-cov__ok, .rep-cov__maybe) { --n: 0; }

    /* ── 05 · WHAT TO FIX FIRST ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       The advice is read before it is labelled. The numeral and the sentence arrive on the row's
       own step; the practice it belongs to follows a fifth of a second behind, so the reader takes
       in what to do and only then which of the six it repairs. */
    .rep[data-anim='on'] .rep-fix__where { --after: 200ms; }

    /* ── 01 · WHAT HAPPENS NEXT ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       Left to right is the order of their clocks: what is already on this page, then what is ready
       now, then when the survey report ships, then the one that only exists for agencies.
       `:nth-child()` above already counts them, and the grid is source-ordered, so the columns
       arrive in the order they are meant to be read in.

       IT COUNTS SOURCE ORDER, NOT VISIBLE ORDER, which is the one thing to know before adding a
       fifth: `#rep-jump` is `[hidden]` until the script reveals it, so on the first paint it is
       still `:nth-child(1)` and everything after it is numbered as though it were there. That is
       harmless — the stagger is a delay, not a position — but a rule that tried to read a column's
       PLACE off this number would be wrong for as long as the card stays hidden. */
  }
</style>

<script>
  import { ANSWERS_KEY, RESULT_KEY } from '../lib/storage';
  import { scoreSurvey } from '../lib/maturity';
  import type { Band } from '../lib/maturity';
  import { answeredCount, confidenceGap, coverage, summarise } from '../lib/report';
  import { DIMENSION_CLAUSES } from '../lib/maturity';
  import { comparison } from '../lib/distribution';
  import type { Answers } from '../lib/questions';

  const KEY = ANSWERS_KEY;

  /**
   * THE STORED RESULT CARRIES ITS OWN SHAPE VERSION, AND THAT IS NOT BOOKKEEPING.
   *
   * This page scores once and keeps the RESULT, throwing the answers away. So when the report grew
   * fields — `answered`, `summary`, `gap`, `cov` — anyone holding a result from the build before
   * that had a value this page could read but not use, and every section backed by a new field
   * hid itself. Silently: the guards are right (a section with no data must not print an empty
   * heading), which is exactly what makes the failure invisible. Reported twice, as "where is the
   * summary" and "I want to see those metrics".
   *
   * Bump this whenever a field is added to what gets stored. A stale shape then reads as no result
   * at all, and the page re-scores from the answers if they are still in hand.
   */
  const RESULT_SHAPE = 3;

  /**
   * ONE EXPRESSION FOR AN ADDRESS, READ BY BOTH CONFIRMATIONS. This is the same test `/survey`
   * applies when it collects the address in the first place. It lived inside the founding-partner
   * block until the send confirmation needed it too; a second copy of a regex literal is a pair of
   * screens that will one day disagree about what an address is, which is the kind of drift nobody
   * finds until somebody's mail does not arrive.
   */
  const EMAIL_RE = /^[^@\s]+@[^@\s]+\.[^@\s]+$/;

  // TWO KEYS, AND THE SECOND ONE IS WHY A REFRESH STILL SHOWS YOUR RESULT.
  //
  // `/survey` hands the answers over and stops clearing them (it used to clear on the way out,
  // which is what made this page always take its empty state). This page scores them once, keeps
  // the RESULT, and throws the answers away. Keeping the answers instead would prefill a second
  // survey started in the same tab, because `/survey` seeds its state from that key on load.
  /**
   * THE STORED RESULT CARRIES THE WHOLE REPORT, NOT JUST THE SCORE.
   *
   * This page scores the answers once and then throws them away, so anything the report needs has
   * to be derived in the same breath. An earlier shape stored only the six dimensions; a result
   * stored by that build lacks the fields below, which is why every read of them is guarded.
   */
  type Result = ReturnType<typeof scoreSurvey> & {
    answered?: number;
    gap?: ReturnType<typeof confidenceGap>;
    cov?: ReturnType<typeof coverage>;
    summary?: ReturnType<typeof summarise>;
    /** Their own address, kept so section 05 can name where the report is going. */
    email?: string;
  };
  let result: Result | null = null;

  try {
    const stored = localStorage.getItem(RESULT_KEY);
    const parsed = stored ? (JSON.parse(stored) as Result & { shape?: number }) : null;
    if (parsed && parsed.shape === RESULT_SHAPE) result = parsed;
  } catch {
    /* fall through to scoring, or to the empty state */
  }

  if (!result) {
    let answers: Answers | null = null;
    try {
      const raw = localStorage.getItem(KEY);
      const parsed = raw ? JSON.parse(raw) : null;
      // An empty object is the same as no answers: nothing was ever recorded to score.
      if (parsed && typeof parsed === 'object' && Object.keys(parsed).length > 0) answers = parsed;
    } catch {
      /* private mode, blocked storage, or a half-written value: the empty state covers all three */
    }

    if (answers) {
      const scored = scoreSurvey(answers);
      result = {
        ...scored,
        answered: answeredCount(answers),
        gap: confidenceGap(answers, scored.band),
        cov: coverage(answers),
        summary: summarise(scored.dimensions, DIMENSION_CLAUSES),
        email: typeof answers.email === 'string' ? answers.email : undefined,
      };
      try {
        localStorage.setItem(RESULT_KEY, JSON.stringify({ shape: RESULT_SHAPE, ...result }));
        localStorage.removeItem(KEY);
      } catch {
        /* storage blocked: the result still renders for this page view */
      }
    }
  }

  if (result) {
    const { score, band, blurb, dimensions, recommendations } = result;

    const scoreEl = document.getElementById('result-score');
    const name = document.getElementById('result-band');
    const line = document.getElementById('result-blurb');
    if (name) {
      name.textContent = band;
      // The band names itself to the stylesheet, which owns the four colours.
      name.dataset.band = band;
    }
    if (line) line.textContent = blurb;

    const compare = document.getElementById('result-compare');
    if (compare) compare.textContent = comparison(band as Band);

    // THE NUMBER COUNTS UP, and the needle travels to it on the same clock. A number that is simply
    // printed reads as a label; one that arrives reads as a measurement being taken, which is what
    // it is. Anyone who has asked for less motion gets the end state immediately.
    const still = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;

    // THE LADDER HANGS ON THIS CLOCK RATHER THAN STARTING A SECOND ONE.
    //
    // The brief asks for the fill and the needle to arrive WITH the final digit. Two timers set to
    // the same duration do not do that: they drift by whatever frame each one happened to start
    // on, and the needle lands a beat off the number it is supposed to be pointing at. So the loop
    // that prints the digit publishes its own eased progress as `--rep-arrive` on the article, and
    // the stylesheet drives both instruments off it. One clock, one easing, no synchronisation.
    const ruled = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.rep');
    const ruledTrack = ruled?.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.bo-ladder__track') ?? null;

    if (scoreEl) {
      if (still || score === 0) {
        scoreEl.textContent = String(score);
      } else {
        const start = performance.now();
        const DURATION = 1100;
        const tick = (now: number) => {
          const t = Math.min(1, (now - start) / DURATION);
          // Ease out, so it decelerates onto the number rather than stopping dead on it.
          const eased = 1 - Math.pow(1 - t, 3);
          scoreEl.textContent = String(Math.round(score * eased));
          if (ruled) {
            // MEASURED ON THE FIRST FRAME, NOT BEFORE IT. `#result` is still `hidden` while this
            // block runs, and a track inside a `hidden` section has no width for the needle to
            // travel across. By the first animation frame the section has been shown.
            if (ruledTrack && !ruled.style.getPropertyValue('--rep-travel')) {
              const across = (ruledTrack.getBoundingClientRect().width * score) / 100;
              ruled.style.setProperty('--rep-travel', `${across}px`);
            }
            ruled.style.setProperty('--rep-arrive', String(eased));
          }
          if (t < 1) requestAnimationFrame(tick);
        };
        requestAnimationFrame(tick);
      }
    }

    document.getElementById('result')?.removeAttribute('hidden');

    /**
     * THE ADVICE ROWS. A numeral, the sentence, and the practice it came out of in that practice's
     * own colour, so a line of advice can always be traced back to the row that produced it.
     *
     * Weakest first, matching how `recommendations` was ordered, so the numeral and the label agree.
     */
    const buildAdvice = (into: HTMLElement) => {
      const weakest = [...dimensions].sort((a, b) => a.score - b.score);
      into.replaceChildren(
        ...recommendations.map((text, i) => {
          const row = document.createElement('div');
          row.className = 'rep-fix';
          const d = weakest[i];
          if (d) row.dataset.score = String(d.score);

          const n = document.createElement('span');
          n.className = 'rep-fix__n';
          n.textContent = String(i + 1);

          const body = document.createElement('p');
          body.className = 'rep-fix__body';
          body.textContent = text;

          const where = document.createElement('span');
          where.className = 'rep-fix__where';
          where.textContent = d ? `${d.label} · ${d.score}/3` : '';

          row.appendChild(n);
          row.appendChild(body);
          row.appendChild(where);
          return row;
        }),
      );
    };

    // ── THE REPORT'S SECTIONS ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    // Every block hides itself when the answer behind it is missing rather than printing an empty
    // heading: a section with nothing under it reads as a fault in the page, not as a gap in the
    // data.
    const show = (id: string) => document.getElementById(id)?.removeAttribute('hidden');

    // The ladder is this page's instrument: one filled track, reading `score` directly, so it
    // cannot drift from the number beside it.
    const fill = document.getElementById('rep-fill');
    const repNeedle = document.getElementById('rep-needle');
    if (fill && repNeedle) {
      repNeedle.hidden = false;
      const settle = () => {
        fill.style.setProperty('--meter-pos', `${score}%`);
        repNeedle.style.setProperty('--meter-pos', `${score}%`);
      };
      if (still) settle();
      else requestAnimationFrame(settle);
      document
        .getElementById('rep-scale')
        ?.querySelector(`[data-band="${band}"]`)
        ?.setAttribute('aria-current', 'step');
    }

    const mailEl = document.getElementById('rep-mail');
    if (mailEl && result.email) {
      mailEl.textContent = result.email;
      document.getElementById('rep-mail-to')?.removeAttribute('hidden');
      // Only now may the column say the address is already on the list, because only now is there
      // an address for the claim to be about.
      document.getElementById('rep-mail-auto')?.removeAttribute('hidden');
    }

    /**
     * ── EMAIL ME MY REPORT ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     *
     * THE ADDRESS AND THE SCORE BOTH COME FROM HERE, AND NEITHER IS REACHABLE SERVER-SIDE TODAY.
     * `/api/submit` is a stub: it stores nothing and hands back the literal token `preview`, so
     * there is no row for the route to look an address up in, and the score is computed in this
     * browser from the answers `/survey` left in localStorage. The page therefore sends what it
     * is already showing and `/api/send-report` validates it. Once responses are persisted (task
     * 2.1) the token is enough and every field below comes off the route instead.
     *
     * IT IS A CONFIRMATION NOW, NOT A SEND. Oleh, 2026-08-28: *"when the user clicks 'Email me my
     * report' they should also see a modal that asks again."* The page button opens `#mail-modal`
     * and only its Confirm posts, which also answers the thing the old one could not: the address
     * is on screen and correctable before the press rather than baked into a `title` attribute.
     */
    const sendWrap = document.getElementById('rep-send-wrap');
    const sendBtn = document.getElementById('rep-send') as HTMLButtonElement | null;
    const sendNote = document.getElementById('rep-send-note');
    const mailModal = document.getElementById('mail-modal') as HTMLDialogElement | null;
    const mailForm = document.getElementById('mail-form');
    const mailGo = document.getElementById('mail-go') as HTMLButtonElement | null;
    const mailCancel = document.getElementById('mail-cancel');
    const mailField = document.getElementById('mail-email') as HTMLInputElement | null;
    const mailErr = document.getElementById('mail-error');

    if (sendWrap && sendBtn && sendNote && mailModal && mailGo && mailField) {
      // THE BUTTON EXISTS WHENEVER THERE IS A SCORE, which is a change: it used to appear only when
      // an address was already stored, because nothing on the page could ask for one. The
      // confirmation asks for one, so the address is prefilled when the survey recorded it and
      // typed when it did not — the same way the founding-partner dialog handles it.
      sendWrap.removeAttribute('hidden');

      // TWO LATCHES, NOT ONE, and both are now on the dialog's Confirm rather than the page's
      // button. `busy` is the double-press guard for the second between the press and the response;
      // `sent` is permanent, because a second copy of the same email is a defect and there is
      // nothing left to confirm once it is in their inbox.
      let busy = false;
      let sent = false;

      const fail = (message: string) => {
        mailField.setAttribute('aria-invalid', 'true');
        if (mailErr) {
          mailErr.textContent = message;
          mailErr.hidden = false;
        }
        mailField.focus();
      };

      const clearFail = () => {
        mailField.removeAttribute('aria-invalid');
        if (mailErr) {
          mailErr.hidden = true;
          mailErr.textContent = '';
        }
      };

      sendBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
        if (sent) return;
        clearFail();
        // Their own address, read back, so the dialog can answer "which address?" before it is
        // asked. Only ever filled when the box is empty: a correction typed and then cancelled
        // survives a reopen.
        if (!mailField.value && result?.email) mailField.value = result.email;
        mailModal.showModal();
      });

      mailCancel?.addEventListener('click', () => mailModal.close('cancel'));
      mailField.addEventListener('input', clearFail);

      // ENTER IN THE FIELD MEANS CONFIRM, NOT CANCEL. Left alone, a `method="dialog"` form closes
      // the dialog on implicit submission, so the one key a reader is most likely to press after
      // correcting their address would have thrown the correction away.
      mailForm?.addEventListener('submit', (event) => {
        event.preventDefault();
        mailGo.click();
      });

      mailGo.addEventListener('click', async () => {
        if (busy || sent) return;
        const to = mailField.value.trim().toLowerCase();
        // CHECKED BEFORE THE DIALOG CLOSES. A bad address caught here is one correction in the box
        // the reader is already looking at; caught after the close it is a red line on a page they
        // have to find their way back into.
        if (!EMAIL_RE.test(to)) {
          fail('That doesn’t look like an email address.');
          return;
        }
        busy = true;
        mailGo.disabled = true;
        mailGo.textContent = 'Sending…';
        clearFail();
        sendNote.removeAttribute('data-state');
        sendNote.textContent = '';
        try {
          const res = await fetch('/api/send-report', {
            method: 'POST',
            headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json' },
            body: JSON.stringify({
              token: mailGo.dataset.token ?? sendBtn.dataset.token ?? 'preview',
              email: to,
              score,
              band,
              blurb,
              dimensions: dimensions.map((d) => ({
                label: d.label,
                answer: d.answer,
                score: d.score,
              })),
              recommendations,
            }),
          });
          const payload = (await res.json().catch(() => null)) as {
            ok?: boolean;
            error?: string;
          } | null;
          if (!res.ok || !payload?.ok) throw new Error(payload?.error ?? String(res.status));
          sent = true;
          mailModal.close('sent');
          sendBtn.disabled = true;
          sendBtn.textContent = 'Sent';
          sendNote.dataset.state = 'sent';
          sendNote.textContent = `On its way to ${to}.`;
        } catch (e) {
          // THE TWO FAILURES HAVE DIFFERENT ANSWERS, and the dialog stays open for the one the
          // reader can actually fix. A rejected address is a correction in the field right there;
          // a provider failure is nothing they can type their way out of, so that one closes and
          // reports on the page, where pressing again is the whole remedy.
          if (e instanceof Error && e.message === 'invalid_email') {
            fail(`We can’t send to ${to}. Check it and confirm again.`);
          } else {
            mailModal.close('failed');
            sendNote.dataset.state = 'failed';
            sendNote.textContent = 'That didn’t send. Try again in a moment — nothing was lost.';
          }
        } finally {
          busy = false;
          mailGo.disabled = false;
          mailGo.textContent = 'Confirm';
        }
      });
    }

    const summaryEl = document.getElementById('rep-summary');
    if (summaryEl && result.summary) {
      summaryEl.textContent = result.summary.text;
      // The pointer to section 04 only appears when there is something for section 04 to say.
      if (result.summary.hasGaps) document.getElementById('rep-trace')?.removeAttribute('hidden');
      document.getElementById('rep-short-sec')?.removeAttribute('hidden');
      // The card in 01 that jumps down here is revealed on this line and no other: it exists only
      // to point at this section, so it may not outlive it. See the comment on `#rep-jump`.
      show('rep-jump');
    }

    const answeredEl = document.getElementById('rep-answered');
    if (answeredEl && typeof result.answered === 'number') {
      answeredEl.textContent = String(result.answered);
      show('rep-facts');
    }

    const gapEl = document.getElementById('rep-gap');
    if (gapEl && result.gap) {
      const { claimed, rungs } = result.gap;
      gapEl.textContent = `You described your own practice as “${claimed}”. Measured against the six practices below, you land at ${band}.`;
      const noteEl = document.getElementById('rep-gap-note');
      if (noteEl) {
        // The bank's own headline chart is the gap between these two, so the page names its size
        // rather than leaving the reader to count rungs.
        noteEl.textContent =
          rungs === 0
            ? 'Your own reading and the measurement agree.'
            : rungs > 0
              ? `${rungs === 1 ? 'One rung' : `${rungs} rungs`} above where the answers put you.`
              : `${rungs === -1 ? 'One rung' : `${Math.abs(rungs)} rungs`} below where the answers put you. You are harder on yourself than the measurement is.`;
      }
      show('rep-gap-sec');
    }

    const table = document.getElementById('rep-table');
    if (table) {
      table.replaceChildren(
        ...dimensions.map((d) => {
          const row = document.createElement('div');
          row.className = 'rep-row';
          row.dataset.score = String(d.score);

          const name = document.createElement('span');
          name.className = 'rep-row__name';
          name.textContent = d.label;

          const said = document.createElement('span');
          said.className = 'rep-row__said';
          said.textContent = d.answer;

          const ticks = document.createElement('span');
          ticks.className = 'rep-row__ticks';
          for (let i = 0; i < 3; i += 1) {
            const tick = document.createElement('span');
            tick.className = 'rep-row__tick';
            tick.dataset.on = String(i < d.score);
            ticks.appendChild(tick);
          }

          const score = document.createElement('span');
          score.className = 'rep-row__score';
          score.textContent = String(d.score);

          row.appendChild(name);
          row.appendChild(said);
          row.appendChild(ticks);
          row.appendChild(score);
          return row;
        }),
      );
      show('rep-table-sec');
    }

    const covYes = document.getElementById('rep-cov-yes');
    const covNo = document.getElementById('rep-cov-no');
    if (covYes && covNo && result.cov) {
      const chip = (text: string, on: boolean) => {
        const el = document.createElement('span');
        el.className = 'rep-chip';
        el.dataset.on = String(on);
        el.textContent = text;
        return el;
      };
      covYes.replaceChildren(...result.cov.captured.map((t) => chip(t, true)));
      covNo.replaceChildren(...result.cov.notConfirmed.map((t) => chip(t, false)));
      show('rep-cov-sec');
    }

    const fix = document.getElementById('rep-fix');
    if (fix && recommendations.length > 0) {
      buildAdvice(fix);
      show('rep-fix-sec');
    }
  }

  /**
   * ── 06 · THE CLOSING SECTION ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   *
   * OUTSIDE `if (result)`, deliberately, and it is the only section that is. Sections 02 to 05 are
   * readings of an answer and have nothing to show without one. This one says the survey happened
   * and offers the link — both true for anyone who reached this page at all, including someone
   * whose stored result failed to parse. It is the last thing on the document and the document
   * should not end on a void for them of all people.
   *
   * THE ADDRESS COMES FROM `location`, not from a constant. It resolves to whatever origin is
   * serving the page, so it is right on the deploy, on a preview and on localhost, and it cannot
   * drift from where the page is actually being read. Today is the argument for it:
   * `survey.baseout.dev` stopped resolving because the domain moved to `survey.ui.baseout.dev`, and
   * a hardcoded constant would have been quietly handing out a dead address from the moment that
   * landed.
   *
   * `origin` AND A TRAILING SLASH, not `href`: the path the reader is standing on is `/thanks`,
   * which is emphatically not where to send anyone. The field shows exactly what the clipboard
   * gets — the same string, not a prettified version of it — because the whole reason for showing
   * a field rather than a bare button is that the value can be checked before it is used, and a
   * field that displays something other than what it copies destroys that.
   *
   * THE CLIPBOARD API CAN FAIL: it needs a secure context and a user gesture, and Safari can still
   * refuse. `select()` on a real input is the entire fallback, which is most of why this is an
   * `<input>` and not a styled `<span>` — the old version had to build a `Range` by hand. The
   * button never claims a copy that did not happen.
   */
  const copyBtn = document.getElementById('rep-copy');
  const urlEl = document.getElementById('rep-url') as HTMLInputElement | null;
  if (copyBtn && urlEl) {
    const link = `${location.origin}/`;
    urlEl.value = link;
    // Not `show()` — that helper is scoped to the `if (result)` block above, and this section is
    // deliberately outside it.
    document.getElementById('rep-end-sec')?.removeAttribute('hidden');

    // A read-only field people are meant to copy: one tap selects the whole thing, so the manual
    // route costs a long-press on a phone rather than a drag across a hostname.
    urlEl.addEventListener('focus', () => urlEl.select());

    copyBtn.addEventListener('click', async () => {
      try {
        await navigator.clipboard.writeText(link);
        // The label and the colour are both CSS off this one attribute, so there is no second
        // place to keep in step and nothing here can leave the button green with the wrong word.
        copyBtn.dataset.state = 'done';
        // Back to the offer, so a second reader of the same screen is not told about a copy that
        // happened before they arrived.
        setTimeout(() => {
          copyBtn.dataset.state = 'idle';
        }, 1800);
      } catch {
        urlEl.focus();
        urlEl.select();
      }
    });
  }

  /**
   * THE LIGHTBOX. Outside the `if (result)` block on purpose: the video is on the page whether or
   * not there is a score to show, so it must open whether or not there is one.
   *
   * `showModal()` does the work — focus trap, inert background, Escape, and focus returned to the
   * button that opened it. What is left is the two things it does not do: a click on the backdrop,
   * which arrives as a click on the dialog itself because the frame does not cover its padding box,
   * and pausing whatever is playing on the way out, which is a no-op today and will not be once the
   * embed lands.
   */
  const lightbox = document.getElementById('video-lightbox') as HTMLDialogElement | null;
  if (lightbox) {
    for (const trigger of document.querySelectorAll('[data-video]')) {
      trigger.addEventListener('click', () => lightbox.showModal());
    }
    document.getElementById('video-close')?.addEventListener('click', () => lightbox.close());
    lightbox.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
      if (event.target === lightbox) lightbox.close();
    });
    lightbox.addEventListener('close', () => {
      // TODO(survey-app task 2.3): stop the embed here, or closing it leaves audio playing behind
      // a dialog nobody can see.
    });
  }


  /**
   * ══ INERTIAL SCROLL ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   *
   * The wheel stops moving the page directly. It moves a TARGET, and the page eases toward that
   * target every frame, so a flick keeps gliding after the fingers stop.
   *
   * WHO IS DELIBERATELY EXCLUDED, AND WHY EACH ONE MATTERS:
   *
   *   · TOUCH. `pointer: coarse` never reaches this. A phone's scrolling is already inertial, tuned
   *     by the platform, and it tracks the finger — replacing it with a lerp makes the page feel
   *     detached from the thumb that is dragging it.
   *   · REDUCED MOTION. Somebody who asked for stillness did not ask for the page to keep moving
   *     after they stopped.
   *   · EVERYTHING THAT IS NOT THE WHEEL. Keyboard, scrollbar drag, find-in-page and
   *     `scrollIntoView` are untouched: `preventDefault` is only ever called on `wheel`. The
   *     listener below resyncs the target whenever the page moved for a reason that was not us, so
   *     pressing space mid-glide does not snap the reader back to where the glide was heading.
   *   · PINCH ZOOM (`ctrlKey`) and the open lightbox, which does its own scrolling.
   *
   * This is the thing I argued against for snap, so the difference is worth stating: this does not
   * decide WHERE the reader ends up, only how quickly they get there, and every other input keeps
   * working exactly as it did. Deleting the block restores the browser's scrolling completely.
   */
  const glideHost = document.querySelector('.rep');
  const coarsePointer = window.matchMedia('(pointer: coarse)').matches;
  const glideStill = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;

  if (glideHost && !coarsePointer && !glideStill) {
    // 0.11 is the fraction of the remaining distance covered each frame: high enough that the page
    // never feels like it is lagging behind the wheel, low enough to read as weight rather than as
    // a delay. Below ~0.08 it swims; above ~0.2 the glide stops being perceptible.
    const EASE = 0.11;
    let target = window.scrollY;
    let frame = 0;
    /** The last position this loop set. Anything else means somebody moved the page, not us. */
    let ours = -1;

    const limit = () => document.documentElement.scrollHeight - window.innerHeight;

    const step = () => {
      const remaining = target - window.scrollY;
      // Half a pixel: below that the browser rounds to the same position and the loop would spin
      // forever moving nothing.
      if (Math.abs(remaining) < 0.5) {
        window.scrollTo(0, target);
        ours = window.scrollY;
        frame = 0;
        return;
      }
      window.scrollTo(0, window.scrollY + remaining * EASE);
      ours = window.scrollY;
      frame = requestAnimationFrame(step);
    };

    window.addEventListener(
      'wheel',
      (event) => {
        if (event.ctrlKey) return;
        if (document.querySelector('dialog[open]')) return;
        event.preventDefault();
        // Some mice report their delta in LINES rather than pixels. Left unconverted, one notch of
        // such a mouse moves the page three pixels.
        const delta = event.deltaMode === 1 ? event.deltaY * 16 : event.deltaY;
        target = Math.max(0, Math.min(limit(), target + delta));
        if (!frame) frame = requestAnimationFrame(step);
      },
      { passive: false },
    );

    /**
     * The page moved and it was not this loop: a key, the scrollbar, a link, find-in-page. Adopt
     * where the reader actually is.
     *
     * COMPARING AGAINST `ours` RATHER THAN AGAINST `frame` IS THE WHOLE POINT, and the first
     * version got it wrong: it only adopted an external scroll while the loop was IDLE, so pressing
     * space or clicking a link mid-glide was overridden a frame later and the reader was dragged
     * back to wherever the glide had been heading. Measured — a `scrollTo(2000)` during a glide
     * ended up back at 600. A scroll this loop caused lands within a pixel of what it just set;
     * anything further away belongs to somebody else.
     */
    window.addEventListener(
      'scroll',
      () => {
        if (!frame || Math.abs(window.scrollY - ours) > 2) target = window.scrollY;
      },
      { passive: true },
    );

    window.addEventListener('resize', () => {
      target = Math.max(0, Math.min(limit(), target));
    });
  }

  /**
   * ══ THE FOUNDING-PARTNER CONFIRMATION ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   *
   * This was one click straight into a green badge. Dan: *"That button just looks dumb, a user can
   * click it by accident and it's not clear what to expect."* Both halves of that are true — an
   * accidental press was a commitment, and nothing on screen said what was being committed to — so
   * the press now opens a dialog and only Confirm posts anything.
   *
   * `showModal()` FOR THE SAME REASONS THE LIGHTBOX USES IT: focus trap, inert background, Escape,
   * and focus returned to the button that opened it. Four behaviours that are tedious to write and
   * easy to get subtly wrong, and this is a dialog that asks somebody to commit to something.
   *
   * OUTSIDE `if (result)` ON PURPOSE. Section 05 renders whether or not there is a score to show,
   * so the button is on the page either way and has to work either way. The address is prefilled
   * from the result when there is one and typed when there is not.
   */
  {
    const modal = document.getElementById('partner-modal') as HTMLDialogElement | null;
    const triggers = [...document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-partner]')];
    const form = document.getElementById('partner-form');
    const go = document.getElementById('partner-go') as HTMLButtonElement | null;
    const cancelBtn = document.getElementById('partner-cancel');
    const field = document.getElementById('partner-email') as HTMLInputElement | null;
    const err = document.getElementById('partner-error');
    const done = document.getElementById('partner-confirmed');

    if (modal && go && field && triggers.length > 0) {
      let busy = false;

      const fail = (message: string) => {
        field.setAttribute('aria-invalid', 'true');
        if (err) {
          err.textContent = message;
          err.hidden = false;
        }
        field.focus();
      };

      const clearFail = () => {
        field.removeAttribute('aria-invalid');
        if (err) {
          err.hidden = true;
          err.textContent = '';
        }
      };

      for (const trigger of triggers) {
        trigger.addEventListener('click', () => {
          clearFail();
          // Their own address, read back, which is the thing the old button never said. Only ever
          // filled when the box is empty: a correction typed and then cancelled survives a reopen.
          if (!field.value && result?.email) field.value = result.email;
          modal.showModal();
        });
      }

      cancelBtn?.addEventListener('click', () => modal.close('cancel'));
      field.addEventListener('input', clearFail);

      // ENTER IN THE FIELD MEANS CONFIRM, NOT CANCEL. Left alone, a `method="dialog"` form closes
      // the dialog on implicit submission, so the one key a reader is most likely to press after
      // correcting their address would have thrown the correction away.
      form?.addEventListener('submit', (event) => {
        event.preventDefault();
        go.click();
      });

      go.addEventListener('click', async () => {
        if (busy) return;
        const email = field.value.trim().toLowerCase();
        if (!EMAIL_RE.test(email)) {
          fail('That doesn’t look like an email address.');
          return;
        }
        busy = true;
        go.disabled = true;
        go.textContent = 'Confirming…';
        clearFail();
        try {
          const res = await fetch('/api/partner-interest', {
            method: 'POST',
            headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json' },
            body: JSON.stringify({ token: go.dataset.token ?? 'preview', email }),
          });
          const payload = (await res.json().catch(() => null)) as { ok?: boolean } | null;
          if (!res.ok || !payload?.ok) throw new Error(String(res.status));
          modal.close('confirmed');
          // The offer is taken, so the way to take it again goes. Every trigger, not just the one
          // that was pressed: there is one on the page today and the attribute allows more.
          for (const trigger of triggers) trigger.hidden = true;
          if (done) done.hidden = false;
        } catch {
          fail('That didn’t go through. Press Confirm again in a moment — nothing was sent.');
        } finally {
          busy = false;
          go.disabled = false;
          go.textContent = 'Confirm';
        }
      });
    }
  }

  /**
   * ══ MOTION ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   *
   * `research/survey-app/animation-brief.md` is the spec. The stylesheet holds every duration,
   * every delay and every start state; what is left for script is the three things CSS cannot
   * decide for itself, and this block is deliberately only those three.
   *
   *   · WHETHER THE HIDDEN STATE MAY EXIST AT ALL. `data-anim` is stamped from here, so a reader
   *     whose JavaScript never arrives is served the finished report rather than an empty page
   *     waiting for an observer that will never run. Written the other way round — hidden in the
   *     markup, revealed by script — a failed script is a blank page, and this page is the only
   *     thing fifteen minutes of someone's afternoon bought them.
   *   · WHEN EACH SECTION HAS ARRIVED. One attribute per section, set once, and the section is
   *     unobserved in the same breath. Scrolling back up finds it already there rather than
   *     replaying at somebody who is trying to re-read it.
   *   · HOW MANY CHIPS THE FIRST COVERAGE COLUMN HOLDS. The only count in the whole reveal that
   *     CSS cannot take for itself, because `:nth-child()` counts within a parent and the two
   *     columns are separate parents.
   *
   * REDUCED MOTION IS CHECKED HERE AND AGAIN IN THE STYLESHEET. Neither guard is load-bearing on
   * its own: the observer is never built, so nothing is ever marked, AND the whole CSS block sits
   * inside `prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference`, so there would be nothing for a mark to
   * trigger. This is the rule people regret getting wrong, so it is written down twice.
   */
  {
    const ruledRep = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>(".rep");
    const ruledStill = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;

    if (ruledRep && !ruledStill) {
      // CAPTURED BEFORE NOT-CONFIRMED, and the offset is a count rather than a guess. A fixed lead
      // for the second column would either overlap the first when a respondent ticked six boxes or
      // leave a hole when they ticked one. The chips are already built by the time this runs.
      const captured = document.getElementById('rep-cov-yes');
      const notConfirmed = document.getElementById('rep-cov-no')?.parentElement;
      if (captured && notConfirmed) {
        notConfirmed.style.setProperty('--base', String(captured.childElementCount));
      }

      ruledRep.dataset.anim = 'on';

      // THE REAR MARGIN IS MEASURED, NOT PICKED. A section's box starts `--rep-slide` above its own
      // heading, so a bare intersection fires while nothing but that padding is on screen and the
      // reveal is spent below the fold. A fifth of the viewport held back puts the trigger at
      // roughly where the heading actually appears, which is also what keeps 01 at rest when the
      // page first paints rather than arriving at a reader who has not scrolled yet.
      const arriving = new IntersectionObserver(
        (entries, self) => {
          for (const entry of entries) {
            if (!entry.isIntersecting) continue;
            (entry.target as HTMLElement).dataset.arrived = 'true';
            self.unobserve(entry.target);
          }
        },
        { rootMargin: '0px 0px -22% 0px' },
      );

      for (const section of ruledRep.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.rep-sec')) {
        arriving.observe(section);
      }
    }
  }
</script>
